(window.__DP=window.__DP||[])[6]=" two-layer membrane indicates monochorionic diamniotic twins. Twin chorionicity is best determined in the first trimester by placental number and membrane features.\",\"A monoamniotic pregnancy cannot have a true intertwin membrane because both fetuses share the same amniotic sac.\",\"Monochorionic monoamniotic twins share both a placenta and amniotic sac, so no dividing membrane is present.\"],\"e\":[\"Twin chorionicity is best determined in the first trimester by placental number and membrane features.\",\"In this patient, one placenta with a thin two-layer membrane indicates monochorionic diamniotic twins.\",\"One placenta with a thin intertwin membrane indicates monochorionic diamniotic twins, meaning one chorion but two amniotic sacs. Monochorionic twins are at risk for vascular anastomoses and twin-twin transfusion syndrome.\"],\"tk\":\"Classify twin pregnancies by placental and membrane findings.\",\"pr\":\"A thin membrane with one placenta suggests MCDA twins.\",\"tp\":\"Determine chorionicity early because surveillance depends on it.\",\"cn\":\"One placenta with a thin intertwin membrane (T sign) indicates monochorionic diamniotic (MCDA) twins.\",\"sl\":\"Monochorionic diamniotic (MCDA): one placenta with shared circulation and a thin membrane with the T sign (thin membrane meeting the placenta at 90 degrees).\",\"s\":[[\"L23_0\",[11,15]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M41\"},{\"id\":\"M42\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":3,\"L\":23,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Monochorionic placental anastomoses\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Placental vascular anastomoses in a monochorionic twin pregnancy are most directly associated with which condition?\",\"o\":[\"Placenta previa\",\"Twin-twin transfusion syndrome\",\"Preterm prelabor rupture of membranes\",\"Shoulder dystocia\",\"Hyperemesis gravidarum\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Placenta previa typically causes painless antepartum bleeding and makes digital vaginal examination unsafe until placental location is defined.\",\"Placental vascular anastomoses cause twin-twin transfusion syndrome. Monochorionic placentas contain vascular anastomoses that can produce unbalanced blood flow between twins.\",\"Preterm prelabour rupture of membranes (PPROM) is rupture of membranes before labor and before 37 weeks; it is not a vascular-anastomosis complication.\",\"Shoulder dystocia is an intrapartum impaction of the fetal shoulder after delivery of the head and is unrelated to placental vascular anastomoses.\",\"Hyperemesis gravidarum is severe pregnancy-related nausea and vomiting and is not caused by monochorionic placental vascular connections.\"],\"e\":[\"Monochorionic placentas contain vascular anastomoses that can produce unbalanced blood flow between twins.\",\"In this patient, placental vascular anastomoses cause twin-twin transfusion syndrome.\",\"This imbalance causes twin-twin transfusion syndrome, with a hypovolemic donor twin and a hypervolemic recipient twin. The condition is unique to monochorionic pregnancies.\"],\"tk\":\"Link monochorionic vascular connections to TTTS.\",\"pr\":\"TTTS requires monochorionic placentation.\",\"tp\":\"Screen monochorionic diamniotic twins serially for amniotic fluid discordance.\",\"cn\":\"Placental vascular anastomoses in monochorionic twins cause twin-twin transfusion syndrome (TTTS) through chronic unbalanced arteriovenous flow.\",\"sl\":\"Shared placenta: vascular anastomoses link the two circulations, causing twin-twin transfusion syndrome (TTTS), twin anaemia-polycythaemia sequence (TAPS) and twin reversed arterial perfusion (TRAP). TTTS: chronic, unbalanced arteriovenous (AV) flow with few or no arterio-arterial (AA) anastomoses.\",\"s\":[[\"L23_0\",[12,32]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M42\"},{\"id\":\"M43\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":3,\"L\":23,\"ty\":[\"Dx\",\"Cause\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"TTTS donor twin\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"In twin-twin transfusion syndrome, one fetus has oligohydramnios and anemia. Which fetus is this?\",\"o\":[\"Acadiac twin\",\"Presenting twin\",\"Recipient fetus\",\"Donor fetus\",\"Twin with polyhydramnios and cardiomegaly\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"An acardiac twin is characteristic of twin reversed arterial perfusion sequence rather than TTTS.\",\"Being the presenting twin describes birth order and does not determine the donor-recipient hemodynamic role.\",\"The recipient twin in TTTS is hypervolemic and typically develops polyhydramnios, with cardiac strain in severe disease.\",\"Oligohydramnios and anemia identify the donor twin. In twin-twin transfusion syndrome, net blood flow passes from the donor twin to the recipient twin through placental vascular anastomoses.\",\"A twin with polyhydramnios and cardiomegaly has the hypervolemic recipient pattern, not the oligohydramnios-anemia pattern described.\"],\"e\":[\"In twin-twin transfusion syndrome, net blood flow passes from the donor twin to the recipient twin through placental vascular anastomoses.\",\"This presentation shows that oligohydramnios and anemia identify the donor twin.\",\"The donor becomes hypovolemic and anemic with reduced urine output, causing oligohydramnios. The recipient becomes hypervolemic and may develop polyhydramnios and cardiac strain.\"],\"tk\":\"Differentiate donor and recipient twins in TTTS.\",\"pr\":\"The donor is dry; the recipient is overloaded.\",\"tp\":\"Use amniotic fluid discordance to identify donor and recipient physiology.\",\"cn\":\"In twin-twin transfusion syndrome (TTTS) the donor twin is hypovolaemic, anaemic and oligohydramniotic.\",\"sl\":\"Donor deepest vertical pocket (DVP) 2 cm or less (oligohydramnios). Quintero stage II: donor bladder not visible, donor severely hypovolaemic.\",\"s\":[[\"L23_0\",[35,36]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M43\"},{\"id\":\"M44\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":3,\"L\":23,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Twin-twin transfusion syndrome\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A monochorionic twin pregnancy shows one twin with oligohydramnios and the other with polyhydramnios. What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"o\":[\"Twin-twin transfusion syndrome\",\"Complete molar pregnancy\",\"Congenital diaphragmatic hernia\",\"Placental abruption\",\"Twin reversed arterial perfusion sequence\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"The oligo-poly sequence in monochorionic twins is TTTS. The classic ultrasound pattern of twin-twin transfusion syndrome is oligohydramnios in the donor sac and polyhydramnios in the recipient sac.\",\"A complete hydatidiform mole has diffuse trophoblastic proliferation, usually no fetal tissue, and often markedly elevated beta-hCG.\",\"Congenital diaphragmatic hernia is a structural fetal anomaly and does not cause the oligohydramnios-polyhydramnios sequence between monochorionic twins.\",\"Placental abruption more often causes painful bleeding, uterine tenderness, or hypertonus.\",\"Twin reversed arterial perfusion sequence involves an acardiac twin perfused by a pump twin and has a different pathophysiology from the donor-recipient fluid imbalance.\"],\"e\":[\"The classic ultrasound pattern of twin-twin transfusion syndrome is oligohydramnios in the donor sac and polyhydramnios in the recipient sac.\",\"In this patient, the oligo-poly sequence in monochorionic twins is TTTS.\",\"It occurs in monochorionic pregnancies because of unbalanced placental vascular connections. Early recognition allows referral for fetal medicine assessment and possible fetoscopic laser therapy.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize the ultrasound pattern of TTTS.\",\"pr\":\"Oligo-poly discordance in monochorionic twins is TTTS until proven otherwise.\",\"tp\":\"Refer suspected TTTS promptly to fetal medicine.\",\"cn\":\"Oligohydramnios in one twin and polyhydramnios in the other in a monochorionic pregnancy is twin-twin transfusion syndrome (TTTS).\",\"sl\":\"Twin-twin transfusion syndrome (TTTS): donor deepest vertical pocket (DVP) 2 cm or less and recipient DVP 8 cm or more up to 20 weeks (10 cm or more after 20 weeks); Quintero stage I is the polyhydramnios-oligohydramnios sequence.\",\"s\":[[\"L23_0\",[35,36]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F352\"},{\"id\":\"M45\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":3,\"L\":23,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Dichorionic diamniotic twins\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Ultrasound in a twin pregnancy shows two fetuses, two separate placentas, and a thick dividing membrane. What is the chorionicity and amnionicity?\",\"o\":[\"Monochorionic monoamniotic\",\"Conjoined twins\",\"Monochorionic twin pregnancy with a single placenta\",\"Dichorionic diamniotic\",\"Monochorionic diamniotic\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Monochorionic monoamniotic twins share both a placenta and amniotic sac, so no dividing membrane is present.\",\"Conjoined twins arise from incomplete division of a monozygotic embryo and are a special monoamniotic presentation.\",\"A single placenta suggests monochorionicity rather than the two separate placentas described.\",\"Two placentas with a thick membrane indicate dichorionic diamniotic twins. Dichorionic diamniotic twins have two chorions and two amniotic sacs.\",\"Monochorionic diamniotic twins share one placenta but have two amniotic sacs separated by a thin membrane.\"],\"e\":[\"Dichorionic diamniotic twins have two chorions and two amniotic sacs.\",\"In this patient, two placentas with a thick membrane indicate dichorionic diamniotic twins.\",\"Ultrasound may show two placentas or a thick intertwin membrane with a lambda or twin-peak sign. This type has lower twin–twin transfusion syndrome (TTTS) risk than monochorionic twins because the placentas are separate.\"],\"tk\":\"Classify DCDA twins using placental number and membrane thickness.\",\"pr\":\"A thick membrane points toward dichorionicity.\",\"tp\":\"Document chorionicity in every twin pregnancy report.\",\"cn\":\"Two separate placentas with a thick dividing membrane indicate dichorionic diamniotic (DCDA) twins.\",\"sl\":\"Dichorionic diamniotic (DCDA): two placentas (may be fused), thick membrane, lambda sign. Two separate placental masses indicate dichorionic (DC); thick membrane DC versus thin monochorionic (MC).\",\"s\":[[\"L23_0\",[11,16]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M45\"},{\"id\":\"M46\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":4,\"ty\":[\"Mx\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Moderate intrahepatic cholestasis delivery timing\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A patient with intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy has total bile acids of 45 micromol/L and stable maternal and fetal status. When is planned delivery generally recommended?\",\"o\":[\"36 weeks\",\"41 weeks\",\"38-39 weeks\",\"34 weeks\",\"Only after spontaneous labor at any gestational age\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"36 weeks is a specific timing or score choice; its appropriateness depends on the stated gestational-age or scoring criteria.\",\"41 weeks is a specific timing or score choice; its appropriateness depends on the stated gestational-age or scoring criteria.\",\"Bile acids of 45 micromol/L fall in the moderate range, commonly managed with planned delivery around 38-39 weeks if stable.\",\"34 weeks is a specific timing or score choice; its appropriateness depends on the stated gestational-age or scoring criteria.\",\"Waiting for spontaneous labor at any gestation ignores the bile-acid-dependent stillbirth risk that informs planned birth timing in intrahepatic cholestasis.\"],\"e\":[\"Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy presents with pruritus, usually on the palms and soles, and elevated bile acids.\",\"In this patient, bile acids of 45 micromol/L fall in the moderate range, commonly managed with planned delivery around 38-39 weeks if stable.\",\"Delivery timing is stratified by bile acid level because stillbirth risk rises with higher bile acids. For bile acids in the 40-99 micromol/L range, planned birth is generally considered around 38-39 weeks in many protocols, with earlier delivery considered when levels are higher or symptoms and comorbidities are concerning.\"],\"tk\":\"Use bile acid level to guide timing of delivery in obstetric cholestasis.\",\"pr\":\"Bile acid level, not transaminase level alone, drives fetal risk stratification.\",\"tp\":\"Repeat bile acids because delivery timing may change if levels rise.\",\"cn\":\"Intrahepatic cholestasis with bile acids 40-99 micromol/L (moderate) is delivered at 38-39 weeks.\",\"sl\":\"Timing of delivery: mild (bile acids 19-39 micromol/L) 40 weeks; moderate (40-99 micromol/L) 38-39 weeks; severe (100 or more) 35-36 weeks.\",\"s\":[[\"L04_0\",[21]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M46\"},{\"id\":\"M47\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":4,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Bile acids in intrahepatic cholestasis\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which laboratory test is most important for diagnosing intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy?\",\"o\":[\"Serum creatine kinase\",\"Serum amylase\",\"Serum alanine aminotransferase\",\"Platelet aggregation study\",\"Serum total bile acids\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Creatine kinase evaluates muscle injury and is unrelated to cholestatic pregnancy disease.\",\"Serum amylase is used when pancreatitis is suspected and does not diagnose intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy.\",\"Alanine aminotransferase may rise in intrahepatic cholestasis, but bile acids are more specific for diagnosis and fetal risk stratification.\",\"Platelet aggregation studies evaluate platelet function disorders and do not diagnose intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy.\",\"Serum bile acids are the key diagnostic test. Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy is diagnosed by pruritus without another clear cause plus elevated serum bile acids, with or without elevated transaminases.\"],\"e\":[\"Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy is diagnosed by pruritus without another clear cause plus elevated serum bile acids, with or without elevated transaminases.\",\"In this patient, serum bile acids are the key diagnostic test.\",\"Bile acid concentration correlates best with fetal risk and guides delivery timing. Liver enzymes may support the diagnosis but are not the defining test.\"],\"tk\":\"Identify the diagnostic laboratory marker for obstetric cholestasis.\",\"pr\":\"Pruritus of palms and soles should prompt bile acid testing.\",\"tp\":\"Do not rely on AST and ALT alone to risk-stratify cholestasis.\",\"cn\":\"Raised serum total bile acids are the key diagnostic and severity test for intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy.\",\"sl\":\"Investigations: liver function tests (LFTs) beyond pregnancy-specific limits (raised alanine aminotransferase [ALT] or aspartate aminotransferase [AST], gamma GT); bile acids, although not necessary to confirm the diagnosis, are useful, especially with typical features but normal LFTs. Delivery timing is graded by bile acid level.\",\"s\":[[\"L04_0\",[16,17,21]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"About the key: The key gives serum total bile acids (E), which matches current Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists (RCOG) 2022 guidance and the slide's bile acid severity grading (L04 page 21). But the slides define cholestasis as pruritus with abnormal liver function tests (LFTs) and say raised bile acids are not needed to confirm the diagnosis (L04 pages 16-17), so serum alanine aminotransferase (ALT, C) can also be defended from the slides.\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M47\"},{\"id\":\"M48\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":4,\"ty\":[\"Prognosis\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"ICP recurrence counseling\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"After intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy, what counseling is appropriate regarding recurrence in future pregnancies?\",\"o\":[\"Recurrence risk is high, often quoted around 45-90%\",\"It recurs only after cesarean delivery\",\"Recurrence risk is approximately 10-20%\",\"It prevents future pregnancy\",\"Recurrence is rare and below 1%\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"The recurrence risk is high. Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy has a high recurrence risk in later pregnancies because genetic, hormonal, and environmental susceptibility may persist.\",\"Cesarean delivery is used when vaginal birth is unsafe or urgent birth is required; maternal stabilization still precedes operative delivery when feasible.\",\"A 10–20% recurrence estimate substantially understates the recurrence risk reported for intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy.\",\"Prior intrahepatic cholestasis does not prevent future pregnancy; future pregnancies need early counseling and repeat testing if symptoms recur.\",\"Recurrence of intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy is common, so counseling that risk is below 1% is inaccurate.\"],\"e\":[\"Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy has a high recurrence risk in later pregnancies because genetic, hormonal, and environmental susceptibility may persist.\",\"In this patient, the recurrence risk is high.\",\"Recurrence estimates vary but are commonly quoted around 45-90%. Patients should be counseled to report pruritus early in future pregnancies.\"],\"tk\":\"Counsel patients with prior obstetric cholestasis about recurrence.\",\"pr\":\"A prior cholestasis history makes itching in the next pregnancy more suspicious.\",\"tp\":\"Arrange early bile acid testing if symptoms recur in a future pregnancy.\",\"cn\":\"Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy has a high recurrence risk in later pregnancies (slides: more than 90%).\",\"sl\":\"Recurrence in subsequent pregnancies exceeds 90%, so liver function tests (LFTs) at booking in future pregnancies are recommended; symptoms may recur with menstruation or the combined oral contraceptive pill (COCP).\",\"s\":[[\"L04_0\",[22]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"About the key: Dr. Moath's slide says recurrence is more than 90%; the option's 45–90% is the range quoted in textbooks. Either way the answer is 'high recurrence'.\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M48\"},{\"id\":\"M49\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":4,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Postpartum follow-up after ICP\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"When should liver function tests usually be rechecked after delivery in a patient with intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy?\",\"o\":[\"6 weeks postpartum\",\"Immediately after cord clamping only\",\"2 weeks postpartum\",\"No postpartum testing is needed\",\"One year postpartum only\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Postpartum reassessment is typically done at about 6 weeks. Symptoms and biochemical abnormalities of intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy usually resolve after delivery.\",\"Testing immediately after cord clamping is too early to document postpartum resolution of cholestasis.\",\"A test at 2 weeks postpartum may still be abnormal during physiologic recovery and is earlier than the standard 6-week reassessment.\",\"Postpartum biochemical confirmation of resolution is recommended because persistent abnormalities suggest another liver disorder.\",\"Waiting one year delays recognition of persistent liver-test abnormalities that warrant further evaluation.\"],\"e\":[\"Symptoms and biochemical abnormalities of intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy usually resolve after delivery.\",\"In this patient, postpartum reassessment is typically done at about 6 weeks.\",\"Postpartum follow-up, commonly around 6 weeks, confirms normalization of liver tests and bile acids and helps identify underlying hepatobiliary disease if abnormalities persist. Persistent abnormal results require further evaluation.\"],\"tk\":\"Plan postpartum follow-up for obstetric cholestasis.\",\"pr\":\"Cholestasis should resolve after birth; persistent abnormalities need investigation.\",\"tp\":\"Check that pruritus and liver tests have resolved at the postpartum visit.\",\"cn\":\"After intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy, liver function tests (LFTs) normalise after delivery and are rechecked postnatally (slides: after 4 weeks).\",\"sl\":\"Following delivery liver function tests (LFTs) return to normal with no permanent liver effect (LFT after 4 weeks).\",\"s\":[[\"L04_0\",[22]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"About the key: The key gives 6 weeks postpartum (A). The slides say to recheck liver function tests (LFTs) after 4 weeks (L04 page 22), which is not an option; 6 weeks (the postnatal review) is the closest and defensible answer, so choose A.\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M49\"},{\"id\":\"M50\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":4,\"ty\":[\"Ix\",\"Prognosis\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Fetal surveillance in ICP\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A patient with intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy is stable, and fetal movements are normal. What is the role of routine cardiotocography (CTG) surveillance in preventing stillbirth?\",\"o\":[\"A normal CTG definitively excludes stillbirth risk\",\"It reliably predicts and prevents stillbirth\",\"It replaces bile acid testing\",\"It is not reliably predictive, so routine CTG alone should not be used to prevent stillbirth\",\"It confirms fetal lung maturity\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A normal CTG cannot definitively exclude later stillbirth risk in intrahepatic cholestasis.\",\"Routine CTG does not reliably predict or prevent stillbirth in intrahepatic cholestasis because deterioration can be sudden.\",\"Serum total bile acids are the key laboratory measurement for diagnosing and risk-stratifying intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy.\",\"Routine CTG is not reliable for preventing cholestasis-related stillbirth. Stillbirth in intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy may be sudden and is not reliably predicted by routine CTG or ultrasound surveillance.\",\"CTG does not assess fetal lung maturity; it evaluates fetal heart-rate patterns.\"],\"e\":[\"Stillbirth in intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy may be sudden and is not reliably predicted by routine CTG or ultrasound surveillance.\",\"In this patient, routine CTG is not reliable for preventing cholestasis-related stillbirth.\",\"Fetal monitoring may be used for usual obstetric indications, but it does not replace bile acid-based risk stratification and planned delivery. Counseling should emphasize fetal movement awareness and timely reassessment.\"],\"tk\":\"Understand the limitations of fetal surveillance in obstetric cholestasis.\",\"pr\":\"A normal CTG does not eliminate cholestasis-related fetal risk.\",\"tp\":\"Use bile acids and gestational age to plan care rather than relying only on CTG.\",\"cn\":\"Cardiotocography (CTG) and ultrasound do not reliably predict or prevent stillbirth in intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy.\",\"sl\":\"No specific method of fetal surveillance can be recommended to predict fetal complications. Ultrasound and cardiotocography (CTG) are not reliable methods for preventing fetal death in obstetric cholestasis; continuous fetal monitoring in labour should be offered.\",\"s\":[[\"L04_0\",[20,23]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F062\"},{\"id\":\"M51\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":1,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Severe preeclampsia stabilization\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"What is a key stabilization step in severe preeclampsia before delivery?\",\"o\":[\"Bed rest alone without antihypertensive therapy\",\"Treat severe hypertension and give magnesium sulfate while assessing maternal labs and fetal status\",\"Perform external cephalic version\",\"Begin estrogen therapy\",\"Delay all treatment until labor starts\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Bed rest alone does not treat severe-range blood pressure or prevent eclamptic seizures.\",\"Stabilization requires blood pressure control, magnesium, and maternal-fetal assessment. Severe preeclampsia requires maternal stabilization before delivery planning.\",\"External cephalic version addresses fetal presentation and is not performed during uncontrolled severe preeclampsia.\",\"Estrogen therapy has no role in acute stabilization of severe preeclampsia.\",\"Delaying treatment despite severe-range hypertension increases the risk of stroke, eclampsia, placental abruption, and maternal organ injury.\"],\"e\":[\"Severe preeclampsia requires maternal stabilization before delivery planning.\",\"This presentation shows that stabilization requires blood pressure control, magnesium, and maternal-fetal assessment.\",\"Stabilization includes urgent treatment of severe blood pressure, magnesium sulfate for seizure prophylaxis, assessment of platelets, renal and liver function, urine output, symptoms, and fetal status. Delivery is definitive, but unsafe transfer to delivery before stabilization increases maternal risk.\"],\"tk\":\"Prioritize stabilization steps in severe preeclampsia.\",\"pr\":\"Stabilize first, deliver second, unless immediate catastrophe forces simultaneous action.\",\"tp\":\"Have a preeclampsia checklist for blood pressure treatment, magnesium, labs, and fluid balance.\",\"cn\":\"Stabilise severe pre-eclampsia with intravenous antihypertensives and magnesium sulphate while assessing maternal laboratory tests and fetal status before delivery.\",\"sl\":\"Management of pre-eclampsia with severe features: admission, intravenous (IV) line, Foley catheter, strict intake/output, IV antihypertensive (+/- magnesium sulphate [MgSO4]), antenatal corticosteroids, laboratory assessment every 6 hours, delivery when the mother is stable.\",\"s\":[[\"L01_0\",[43,44,45]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M51\"},{\"id\":\"M52\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":1,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Magnesium toxicity antidote\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A patient receiving magnesium sulfate develops toxicity. Which medication is the antidote?\",\"o\":[\"Naloxone\",\"Calcium gluconate\",\"Flumazenil\",\"Protamine sulfate\",\"Vitamin K\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Naloxone reverses opioid toxicity and has no role in magnesium toxicity.\",\"Calcium gluconate reverses magnesium toxicity. Magnesium toxicity may cause loss of deep tendon reflexes, respiratory depression, hypotension, and cardiac arrest.\",\"Flumazenil antagonizes benzodiazepines and is not the antidote for magnesium toxicity.\",\"Protamine sulfate neutralizes unfractionated heparin and partially reverses low-molecular-weight heparin.\",\"Vitamin K treats vitamin-K deficiency and reverses warfarin-associated coagulopathy, not magnesium toxicity.\"],\"e\":[\"Magnesium toxicity may cause loss of deep tendon reflexes, respiratory depression, hypotension, and cardiac arrest.\",\"In this patient, calcium gluconate reverses magnesium toxicity.\",\"The antidote is intravenous calcium gluconate, given with immediate supportive care and cessation of magnesium infusion. Renal impairment increases the risk of toxicity because magnesium is renally excreted.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize and treat magnesium sulfate toxicity.\",\"pr\":\"Loss of reflexes is an early warning sign of magnesium toxicity.\",\"tp\":\"Keep calcium gluconate immediately available wherever magnesium is infused.\",\"cn\":\"Calcium gluconate (1 g intravenously) is the antidote for magnesium sulphate toxicity.\",\"sl\":\"If magnesium toxicity develops: stop magnesium sulphate, check vital signs and plasma levels, and administer 1 g calcium gluconate intravenously (IV) over 3 minutes, considering diuretics.\",\"s\":[[\"L01_0\",[47]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F349\"},{\"id\":\"M53\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":1,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Delivery after severe preeclampsia\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"After maternal stabilization for preeclampsia with severe features and an indication for birth, what is the definitive management?\",\"o\":[\"Delivery, with route based on obstetric indications\",\"Radiation treatment\",\"Continue pregnancy indefinitely\",\"Methotrexate therapy\",\"Cesarean delivery in every patient regardless of obstetric factors\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Definitive management after stabilization is delivery. Delivery is the definitive treatment for preeclampsia, although timing depends on gestational age, severity, maternal status, and fetal status.\",\"Pelvic radiotherapy is not routine first-line therapy for low-risk gestational trophoblastic neoplasia.\",\"Continuing pregnancy indefinitely is unsafe once a clear indication for birth has developed.\",\"Methotrexate is used for selected ectopic pregnancies and low-risk gestational trophoblastic neoplasia, not for routine miscarriage or urinary symptoms.\",\"Cesarean delivery is used when vaginal birth is unsafe or urgent birth is required; maternal stabilization still precedes operative delivery when feasible.\"],\"e\":[\"Delivery is the definitive treatment for preeclampsia, although timing depends on gestational age, severity, maternal status, and fetal status.\",\"In this patient, definitive management after stabilization is delivery.\",\"In severe disease with an indication for birth, the mother should be stabilized with antihypertensives and magnesium sulfate before delivery when possible. The route of delivery is determined by obstetric factors rather than preeclampsia alone.\"],\"tk\":\"Know when delivery is definitive management in severe preeclampsia.\",\"pr\":\"Cesarean is not automatic; the route depends on obstetric circumstances.\",\"tp\":\"Stabilize maternal blood pressure and seizures before induction or cesarean whenever possible.\",\"cn\":\"Delivery is the definitive treatment of pre-eclampsia after maternal stabilisation; route depends on obstetric factors.\",\"sl\":\"Definitive management for gestational hypertension, pre-eclampsia and eclampsia is delivery because the placenta is the insulting agent. Delivery is usually by caesarean section (CS), unless mother and fetus are stable and the cervix favourable, when induction of labour (IOL) with vaginal prostaglandins can be tried.\",\"s\":[[\"L01_0\",[40,43,49]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F351\"},{\"id\":\"M54\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":1,\"ty\":[\"Dx\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Severe-feature symptoms in preeclampsia\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which symptom is a severe feature of preeclampsia?\",\"o\":[\"Isolated dependent ankle edema\",\"Physiologic nausea in early pregnancy\",\"Sudden severe headache with blurred vision\",\"Round ligament pain\",\"Mild ankle edema only\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Dependent ankle edema is common in normal pregnancy and is not by itself a severe feature of preeclampsia.\",\"Physiologic first-trimester nausea is common and, without severe hypertension or organ dysfunction, is not a severe feature.\",\"New severe headache with visual symptoms is a severe feature. Severe features of preeclampsia include severe-range blood pressure, thrombocytopenia, renal insufficiency, impaired liver function with pain, pulmonary edema, and new-onset cerebral or visual symptoms.\",\"Round-ligament pain is a benign musculoskeletal symptom and is not a severe feature; it lacks neurologic or end-organ severe features.\",\"Dependent ankle edema is common in normal pregnancy and is not by itself a severe feature of preeclampsia.\"],\"e\":[\"Severe features of preeclampsia include severe-range blood pressure, thrombocytopenia, renal insufficiency, impaired liver function with pain, pulmonary edema, and new-onset cerebral or visual symptoms.\",\"This presentation shows that new severe headache with visual symptoms is a severe feature.\",\"Sudden severe headache with blurred vision suggests cerebral involvement and risk of eclampsia or stroke. It requires urgent evaluation and treatment.\"],\"tk\":\"Identify neurologic severe features of preeclampsia.\",\"pr\":\"Headache plus visual symptoms in hypertension is dangerous until proven otherwise.\",\"tp\":\"Ask specifically about headache, visual symptoms, epigastric pain, and dyspnea in every hypertensive pregnant patient.\",\"cn\":\"Persistent severe headache or visual disturbance (blurred vision, scotomata) is a severe feature of pre-eclampsia; oedema is not.\",\"sl\":\"Signs and symptoms of severe pre-eclampsia: cerebral or visual disturbances (persistent headache, blurred vision, scotomata), persistent epigastric or right upper quadrant pain, and pulmonary oedema.\",\"s\":[[\"L01_0\",[37]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M54\"},{\"id\":\"M55\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":1,\"ty\":[\"Complication\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Fatal complications of eclampsia\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A patient with eclampsia becomes unconscious with nonreactive pupils. Which cause of death is most likely among the following?\",\"o\":[\"Ovarian torsion\",\"Uncomplicated cystitis\",\"Iron deficiency anemia\",\"Intracranial hemorrhage\",\"Acute pyelonephritis\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Ovarian torsion causes acute pelvic pain and vomiting rather than catastrophic neurologic deterioration.\",\"Uncomplicated cystitis causes lower urinary symptoms and does not cause acute neurologic collapse.\",\"Iron-deficiency anemia causes fatigue and pallor but does not explain sudden coma with nonreactive pupils after eclampsia; the expected picture is lower urinary tract symptoms without acute neurologic collapse.\",\"Coma with nonreactive pupils in eclampsia suggests intracranial hemorrhage. Severe hypertension in preeclampsia and eclampsia can lead to cerebrovascular catastrophe, including intracranial hemorrhage.\",\"Pyelonephritis may cause fever, flank pain, and sepsis but does not specifically explain sudden fixed pupils after an eclamptic event.\"],\"e\":[\"Severe hypertension in preeclampsia and eclampsia can lead to cerebrovascular catastrophe, including intracranial hemorrhage.\",\"In this patient, coma with nonreactive pupils in eclampsia suggests intracranial hemorrhage.\",\"Loss of consciousness with nonreactive pupils is concerning for major intracranial bleeding or herniation. Rapid blood pressure control, seizure treatment, and neurologic emergency management are critical.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize fatal neurologic complications of eclampsia.\",\"pr\":\"Maternal stroke prevention is the reason severe hypertension is treated urgently.\",\"tp\":\"Call anesthesia, ICU, and neurology/neurosurgery early when neurologic signs persist after eclamptic seizure.\",\"cn\":\"Intracranial (cerebral) haemorrhage from uncontrolled severe hypertension is a leading direct cause of maternal death in pre-eclampsia and eclampsia.\",\"sl\":\"Maternal complications of pre-eclampsia include cerebrovascular accidents; eclamptic seizures may occur when mean arterial pressure exceeds cerebral autoregulation, causing cerebral oedema and raised intracranial pressure.\",\"s\":[[\"L01_0\",[50,53]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":4,\"rs\":\"F099\"},{\"id\":\"M56\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":8,\"L\":37,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Conservative prolapse management\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 74-year-old sexually inactive woman has pelvic organ prolapse beyond the introitus and does not want surgery. What is the most appropriate initial management?\",\"o\":[\"Vaginal pessary\",\"Botulinum toxin injection\",\"Endometrial biopsy\",\"Observation alone despite symptomatic prolapse\",\"Sacrocolpopexy\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A pessary is the best initial nonsurgical treatment for symptomatic prolapse. Pelvic organ prolapse management depends on symptoms, prolapse compartment, sexual activity, comorbidities, and patient preference.\",\"Intradetrusor botulinum toxin A is an escalation option for refractory overactive bladder after conservative and medication therapy.\",\"Endometrial biopsy samples the uterine lining for hyperplasia or malignancy when age, risk factors, or persistent abnormal bleeding warrant sampling.\",\"Observation alone is unlikely to relieve symptomatic prolapse extending beyond the introitus when the patient is seeking conservative treatment.\",\"Sacrocolpopexy is a reconstructive operation for apical or vault prolapse that preserves vaginal coital function.\"],\"e\":[\"Pelvic organ prolapse management depends on symptoms, prolapse compartment, sexual activity, comorbidities, and patient preference.\",\"In this patient, a pessary is the best initial nonsurgical treatment for symptomatic prolapse.\",\"A vaginal pessary is an appropriate nonsurgical option for symptomatic prolapse, especially in patients who decline surgery or are poor surgical candidates. Follow-up is needed to monitor fit, discharge, ulceration, and ability to self-care.\"],\"tk\":\"Choose prolapse treatment based on symptoms and patient goals.\",\"pr\":\"A pessary can control prolapse symptoms without surgery.\",\"tp\":\"Check pessary fit and vaginal mucosa regularly after insertion.\",\"cn\":\"Symptomatic pelvic organ prolapse (POP) in a woman declining surgery is managed conservatively with a vaginal pessary.\",\"sl\":\"Options of management: no treatment (pelvic floor exercise); conservative, such as physiotherapy or pessary; surgical treatment.\",\"s\":[[\"L37_0\",[47]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M56\"},{\"id\":\"M57\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":8,\"L\":36,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"First-line overactive bladder treatment\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 69-year-old woman has urgency, frequency, and urge urinary incontinence without infection or other pathology. What is the most appropriate initial management?\",\"o\":[\"Immediate antimuscarinic drug therapy\",\"Immediate cystectomy\",\"Sacrocolpopexy\",\"Anterior colporrhaphy\",\"Bladder training\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Antimuscarinic drugs are appropriate after behavioral measures are inadequate, but behavioral voiding retraining is first-line for uncomplicated urgency-predominant symptoms.\",\"Laparoscopic cystectomy is preferred over simple drainage for a symptomatic endometrioma when surgery is indicated because recurrence is lower.\",\"Sacrocolpopexy is a reconstructive operation for apical or vault prolapse that preserves vaginal coital function.\",\"Anterior colporrhaphy repairs anterior vaginal wall prolapse and does not treat urgency caused by overactive bladder.\",\"Bladder training is the initial treatment for uncomplicated overactive bladder. Overactive bladder syndrome is urgency, usually with frequency and nocturia, with or without urge incontinence, in the absence of infection or other pathology.\"],\"e\":[\"Overactive bladder syndrome is urgency, usually with frequency and nocturia, with or without urge incontinence, in the absence of infection or other pathology.\",\"In this patient, bladder training is the initial treatment for uncomplicated overactive bladder.\",\"Initial therapy is conservative and includes bladder training, fluid and caffeine advice, and lifestyle measures. Medications are added if conservative treatment fails.\"],\"tk\":\"Treat overactive bladder first with behavioral therapy.\",\"pr\":\"Urgency symptoms respond best initially to bladder training.\",\"tp\":\"Exclude infection before labeling symptoms as overactive bladder.\",\"cn\":\"Bladder training is first-line management of overactive bladder (OAB) with urge incontinence before antimuscarinic drugs.\",\"sl\":\"National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE): bladder retraining for a minimum of 6 weeks; if no satisfactory benefit, antimuscarinics, first-line immediate-release oxybutynin.\",\"s\":[[\"L36_0\",[76,84]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"M16\"},{\"id\":\"M58\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":8,\"L\":36,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Surgery for stress urinary incontinence\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 55-year-old woman has stress urinary incontinence despite adequate conservative treatment. Which management is most appropriate to discuss next?\",\"o\":[\"Intravesical botulinum toxin\",\"Antibiotics for six months\",\"Surgical management such as a mid-urethral sling\",\"Bladder training alone\",\"Vaginal estrogen as sole definitive therapy\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Intradetrusor botulinum toxin A is an escalation option for refractory overactive bladder after conservative and medication therapy.\",\"Long-term antibiotics are not treatment for stress urinary incontinence in the absence of infection.\",\"Persistent bothersome stress incontinence after conservative therapy can be managed surgically.\",\"Bladder training is first-line behavioral treatment for urgency-predominant urinary incontinence and overactive bladder.\",\"Vaginal estrogen can improve genitourinary menopausal symptoms but is not definitive surgical treatment for persistent stress incontinence.\"],\"e\":[\"Stress urinary incontinence is leakage with increased intra-abdominal pressure due to urethral hypermobility or sphincter deficiency.\",\"In this patient, persistent bothersome stress incontinence after conservative therapy can be managed surgically.\",\"First-line treatment is pelvic floor muscle training, but persistent bothersome symptoms after conservative therapy warrant discussion of surgical options such as mid-urethral sling, colposuspension, or autologous fascial sling depending on local guidance and patient factors. Duloxetine may be considered when surgery is declined or unsuitable.\"],\"tk\":\"Escalate stress urinary incontinence after failed pelvic floor training.\",\"pr\":\"Botulinum toxin treats urgency, not stress leakage.\",\"tp\":\"Clarify whether leakage is stress-predominant before offering surgery.\",\"cn\":\"Stress urinary incontinence (SUI) failing conservative treatment is managed surgically, for example with a mid-urethral sling or colposuspension.\",\"sl\":\"National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE): retropubic tapes recommended where conservative management failed; colposuspension a recommended alternative. Key points: conservative management before surgery; mid-urethral tape used to be the operation of choice, now colposuspension is the first choice.\",\"s\":[[\"L36_0\",[73,74]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M58\"},{\"id\":\"M59\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":8,\"L\":36,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"First-line stress incontinence therapy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 42-year-old woman has urine leakage when coughing and sneezing and has received no previous treatment. What is the most appropriate initial management?\",\"o\":[\"Long-term catheterization\",\"Intradetrusor botulinum toxin\",\"Supervised pelvic floor muscle training for at least 3 months\",\"Immediate hysterectomy\",\"Bladder training alone\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Long-term catheterization is inappropriate first-line treatment for uncomplicated stress urinary incontinence and carries infection and urethral risks.\",\"Intradetrusor botulinum toxin A is an escalation option for refractory overactive bladder after conservative and medication therapy.\",\"New stress urinary incontinence is initially treated with pelvic floor muscle training. Stress urinary incontinence is leakage during physical exertion, coughing, or sneezing.\",\"Hysterectomy is major surgery reserved for selected definitive indications and is not first-line for uncomplicated functional disease.\",\"Bladder training is first-line behavioral treatment for urgency-predominant urinary incontinence and overactive bladder.\"],\"e\":[\"Stress urinary incontinence is leakage during physical exertion, coughing, or sneezing.\",\"In this patient, new stress urinary incontinence is initially treated with pelvic floor muscle training.\",\"Initial treatment is supervised pelvic floor muscle training for at least 3 months, with lifestyle advice such as weight reduction if relevant. Surgery is reserved for persistent bothersome symptoms after conservative therapy.\"],\"tk\":\"Select first-line therapy for stress urinary incontinence.\",\"pr\":\"Cough leakage points to stress urinary incontinence.\",\"tp\":\"Demonstrate pelvic floor contraction technique rather than only telling the patient to do exercises.\",\"cn\":\"Untreated stress urinary incontinence (SUI) is managed first with supervised pelvic floor muscle training (PFMT) for at least 3 months.\",\"sl\":\"Pelvic floor muscle training (PFMT) of at least three months should be offered as first-line treatment to all women with stress (SUI) or mixed urinary incontinence (MUI); 8 contractions 3 times per day.\",\"s\":[[\"L36_0\",[57,73]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":4,\"rs\":\"F007\"},{\"id\":\"M60\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":8,\"L\":37,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Vaginal vault prolapse surgery\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 60-year-old sexually active woman has vaginal vault prolapse after hysterectomy and wants definitive surgical treatment. What is the most appropriate management?\",\"o\":[\"Cervical cerclage\",\"Colpocleisis\",\"Sacrocolpopexy\",\"Oxybutynin\",\"Bladder training\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Cervical cerclage supports a cervix at risk of preterm dilation and is not a treatment for hemorrhage or urinary symptoms.\",\"Colpocleisis is an obliterative prolapse operation generally reserved for women who no longer desire vaginal intercourse.\",\"Sacrocolpopexy is appropriate definitive surgery for sexually active vault prolapse. Vaginal vault prolapse is descent of the vaginal apex after hysterectomy.\",\"Oxybutynin is an antimuscarinic drug for overactive bladder; dry mouth, constipation, and cognitive adverse effects can limit use.\",\"Bladder training is first-line behavioral treatment for urgency-predominant urinary incontinence and overactive bladder.\"],\"e\":[\"Vaginal vault prolapse is descent of the vaginal apex after hysterectomy.\",\"In this patient, sacrocolpopexy is appropriate definitive surgery for sexually active vault prolapse.\",\"In sexually active patients desiring definitive reconstructive surgery, sacrocolpopexy provides apical support while preserving vaginal capacity. Obliterative procedures are generally reserved for patients who no longer desire vaginal intercourse.\"],\"tk\":\"Match prolapse surgery to compartment and sexual function goals.\",\"pr\":\"Vault prolapse needs apical support, not only anterior wall repair.\",\"tp\":\"Ask about sexual activity before choosing reconstructive versus obliterative prolapse surgery.\",\"cn\":\"Sacrocolpopexy is the definitive abdominal or laparoscopic operation for post-hysterectomy vault prolapse that preserves sexual function.\",\"sl\":\"Aims of prolapse surgery include restoring anatomy and preserving sexual function. Secondary (vault) procedures: vaginal sacrospinous, iliococcygeus or uterosacral fixation; abdominal or laparoscopic sacrocolpopexy.\",\"s\":[[\"L37_0\",[50,51]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M60\"},{\"id\":\"M61\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":4,\"L\":18,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"PPROM biochemical diagnosis\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 30-year-old at 32 weeks has a gush of fluid and persistent leakage, but no pooling is seen on sterile speculum examination. Which test best confirms ruptured membranes?\",\"o\":[\"Ferning test\",\"PAMG-1 test\",\"Digital vaginal examination\",\"Nitrazine test\",\"Complete blood count\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Ferning can support membrane rupture but is less useful when no fluid can be sampled on speculum examination.\",\"PAMG-1 testing helps confirm rupture when pooling is not obvious. Preterm prelabour rupture of membranes (PPROM) is rupture of membranes before labor and before 37 weeks.\",\"Digital vaginal examination should be avoided in PPROM unless delivery is imminent because it shortens latency and increases infection risk.\",\"Nitrazine testing detects alkaline vaginal fluid but can be falsely positive with blood, semen, or infection.\",\"A complete blood count can support infection assessment but does not confirm whether membranes have ruptured.\"],\"e\":[\"PPROM is rupture of membranes before labor and before 37 weeks.\",\"In this patient, pAMG-1 testing helps confirm rupture when pooling is not obvious.\",\"When pooling is absent and the diagnosis is uncertain, placental alpha microglobulin-1 testing is a highly useful immunoassay for amniotic fluid proteins. Digital vaginal examination should be avoided because it increases infection risk unless delivery is imminent.\"],\"tk\":\"Confirm suspected PPROM while avoiding unnecessary digital examination.\",\"pr\":\"No pooling does not exclude PPROM.\",\"tp\":\"Use sterile speculum examination and avoid digital examination in suspected PPROM.\",\"cn\":\"When no pooling is seen, a placental alpha microglobulin-1 (PAMG-1) test (AmniSure) confirms ruptured membranes.\",\"sl\":\"The AmniSure test detects the placental alpha microglobulin-1 (PAMG-1) protein marker, with 98.9% sensitivity and 100% specificity, gives results within minutes and has no gestational age limitation; it is used in the absence of demonstrable fluid loss.\",\"s\":[[\"L18_0\",[9,10]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F518\"},{\"id\":\"M62\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":4,\"L\":18,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\",\"Dx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Definition of PPROM\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 30-year-old at 32 weeks has sudden fluid leakage before contractions. What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"o\":[\"Preterm labor with intact membranes\",\"Term prelabour rupture of membranes (PROM)\",\"Placental abruption\",\"Inevitable miscarriage\",\"Preterm prelabor rupture of membranes\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Preterm labor with intact membranes requires regular contractions with cervical change while the membranes remain unruptured.\",\"Term PROM is rupture before labor at 37 weeks or later.\",\"Placental abruption more often causes painful bleeding, uterine tenderness, or hypertonus.\",\"Inevitable miscarriage presents with bleeding and an open cervix before expulsion is complete.\",\"Fluid leakage before labor at 32 weeks is PPROM. Preterm prelabor rupture of membranes is rupture of fetal membranes before the onset of labor and before 37 weeks.\"],\"e\":[\"Preterm prelabor rupture of membranes is rupture of fetal membranes before the onset of labor and before 37 weeks.\",\"In this patient, fluid leakage before labor at 32 weeks is PPROM.\",\"The typical history is a sudden gush of fluid followed by continuous leakage. It is important because management balances prematurity risks against infection risks.\"],\"tk\":\"Define PPROM by gestational age and timing relative to labor.\",\"pr\":\"PROM before 37 weeks is PPROM.\",\"tp\":\"Ask gestational age first when classifying membrane rupture.\",\"cn\":\"Rupture of membranes before labour and before 37 weeks is preterm prelabour rupture of membranes (PPROM).\",\"sl\":\"Prelabour rupture of membranes (PROM) is rupture of membranes prior to the onset of labour, at term (37 weeks or more) or preterm (before 37 weeks).\",\"s\":[[\"L18_0\",[2]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M62\"},{\"id\":\"M63\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":4,\"L\":18,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Expectant PPROM management\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A stable patient with confirmed preterm prelabour rupture of membranes (PPROM) at 32 weeks has no contractions and no signs of infection. What is the most appropriate management?\",\"o\":[\"Magnesium sulfate alone without latency antibiotics\",\"Antibiotics and antenatal corticosteroids with expectant management\",\"Digital vaginal examinations every 4 hours\",\"Tocolytics only\",\"Immediate delivery\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Magnesium sulfate prevents and treats eclamptic seizures and can provide fetal neuroprotection before early preterm birth.\",\"Stable PPROM at 32 weeks is managed with antibiotics and corticosteroids. Stable PPROM before 34 weeks is usually managed expectantly if there is no infection, abruption, labor, or nonreassuring fetal status.\",\"Repeated digital vaginal examinations increase ascending infection risk and can shorten latency after PPROM.\",\"Tocolysis alone does not provide latency antibiotic coverage or fetal maturation benefit and is not routine management of PPROM.\",\"Immediate delivery is not routine at 32 weeks when PPROM is stable and there is no infection, labor, abruption, or fetal compromise.\"],\"e\":[\"Stable PPROM before 34 weeks is usually managed expectantly if there is no infection, abruption, labor, or nonreassuring fetal status.\",\"In this patient, stable PPROM at 32 weeks is managed with antibiotics and corticosteroids.\",\"Management includes antenatal corticosteroids to reduce prematurity complications and latency antibiotics to prolong pregnancy and reduce infection. Maternal and fetal monitoring is essential.\"],\"tk\":\"Manage PPROM at 32 weeks using expectant care and prematurity prevention.\",\"pr\":\"At 32 weeks, the goal is to gain safe latency while watching for infection.\",\"tp\":\"Fever, uterine tenderness, or fetal tachycardia changes management toward delivery.\",\"cn\":\"Stable preterm prelabour rupture of membranes (PPROM) is managed expectantly with latency antibiotics (erythromycin) and corticosteroids; tocolysis is not recommended.\",\"sl\":\"Antibiotics prolong the latency period (erythromycin 250 mg four times daily for 10 days); tocolysis is not recommended; corticosteroids are given. Preterm prelabour rupture of membranes (PPROM) without contraindications is managed expectantly until 37+0 weeks.\",\"s\":[[\"L18_0\",[13,14]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M63\"},{\"id\":\"M64\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":4,\"L\":19,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Antenatal corticosteroid effects\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Antenatal corticosteroids in preterm prelabour rupture of membranes (PPROM) reduce all of the following neonatal complications EXCEPT:\",\"o\":[\"Respiratory distress syndrome\",\"Neonatal mortality\",\"Intraventricular hemorrhage\",\"Necrotizing enterocolitis\",\"Cerebral palsy\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Antenatal corticosteroids reduce respiratory distress syndrome by accelerating fetal lung maturation.\",\"Antenatal corticosteroids reduce neonatal mortality in pregnancies at risk of preterm birth.\",\"Antenatal corticosteroids reduce intraventricular hemorrhage in infants at risk of preterm birth.\",\"Antenatal corticosteroids reduce necrotizing enterocolitis and other complications of prematurity.\",\"Reduction of cerebral palsy (fetal neuroprotection) in early preterm birth comes from magnesium sulfate, not corticosteroids. Antenatal corticosteroids accelerate fetal lung maturation and reduce respiratory distress syndrome, intraventricular hemorrhage, necrotizing enterocolitis, and neonatal mortality.\"],\"e\":[\"Antenatal corticosteroids accelerate fetal lung maturation and reduce respiratory distress syndrome, intraventricular hemorrhage, necrotizing enterocolitis, and neonatal mortality in pregnancies at risk of preterm birth.\",\"This presentation shows that fetal neuroprotection is provided by magnesium sulfate, not corticosteroids.\",\"Fetal neuroprotection, particularly reduction of cerebral palsy risk before early preterm delivery, is the role of magnesium sulfate rather than corticosteroids. The distinction is commonly tested.\"],\"tk\":\"Differentiate benefits of antenatal corticosteroids from magnesium sulfate.\",\"pr\":\"Steroids mature lungs; magnesium protects the preterm brain.\",\"tp\":\"When giving both steroids and magnesium, explain the different purpose of each medication.\",\"cn\":\"Fetal neuroprotection against cerebral palsy comes from magnesium sulphate, not antenatal corticosteroids.\",\"sl\":\"Corticosteroids decrease respiratory distress syndrome (RDS), neonatal mortality and intraventricular haemorrhage. Magnesium sulphate given shortly before delivery reduces the risk of cerebral palsy in preterm infants.\",\"s\":[[\"L19_0\",[31,37]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M64\"},{\"id\":\"M65\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":4,\"L\":18,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Chorioamnionitis after PPROM\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A patient admitted with preterm prelabour rupture of membranes (PPROM) develops fever, uterine tenderness, and fetal tachycardia. What is the most appropriate management?\",\"o\":[\"Continue expectant management\",\"Amniocentesis before starting treatment\",\"Start tocolytics\",\"Administer magnesium sulfate only\",\"Prompt delivery with broad-spectrum antibiotics\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Expectant management is contraindicated once chorioamnionitis is suspected because ongoing pregnancy increases maternal and neonatal sepsis risk.\",\"Amniocentesis is not required before treating a clinically apparent intra-amniotic infection with maternal fever, uterine tenderness, and fetal tachycardia.\",\"Tocolysis is contraindicated in intra-amniotic infection because delaying delivery prolongs exposure to infection.\",\"Magnesium sulfate prevents and treats eclamptic seizures and can provide fetal neuroprotection before early preterm birth.\",\"PPROM with chorioamnionitis requires antibiotics and prompt delivery. Fever, uterine tenderness, and fetal tachycardia after PPROM indicate intra-amniotic infection or chorioamnionitis.\"],\"e\":[\"Fever, uterine tenderness, and fetal tachycardia after PPROM indicate intra-amniotic infection or chorioamnionitis.\",\"In this patient, pPROM with chorioamnionitis requires antibiotics and prompt delivery.\",\"This is an indication for broad-spectrum antibiotics and prompt delivery because continuing expectant management increases maternal and neonatal sepsis risk. Tocolysis is contraindicated when infection is suspected.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize chorioamnionitis as an indication for delivery in PPROM.\",\"pr\":\"Infection ends expectant management in PPROM.\",\"tp\":\"Do not give tocolysis when chorioamnionitis is suspected.\",\"cn\":\"Chorioamnionitis complicating preterm prelabour rupture of membranes (PPROM) requires broad-spectrum antibiotics and prompt delivery.\",\"sl\":\"Chorioamnionitis is a major risk of preterm prelabour rupture of membranes (PPROM); it is diagnosed by clinical features (fever, lower abdominal pain, abnormal discharge), C-reactive protein (CRP), white cell count and fetal heart rate.\",\"s\":[[\"L18_0\",[11,12]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M65\"},{\"id\":\"M66\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":4,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Physiologic liver tests in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about physiological liver changes in pregnancy is false?\",\"o\":[\"Serum albumin decreases\",\"Serum alkaline phosphatase increases\",\"Serum bilirubin is usually unchanged\",\"All liver enzymes normally decrease\",\"Serum aspartate aminotransferase (AST) and alanine aminotransferase (ALT) usually remain within the nonpregnant reference range\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Serum albumin decreases physiologically in pregnancy because of hemodilution.\",\"Alkaline phosphatase rises in pregnancy because of placental production and is not a reliable marker of hepatobiliary disease by itself.\",\"Serum bilirubin is usually unchanged or slightly lower in uncomplicated pregnancy.\",\"It is false that all liver enzymes normally decrease. Normal pregnancy changes several liver-related laboratory values.\",\"AST and ALT generally remain within normal nonpregnant ranges during uncomplicated pregnancy.\"],\"e\":[\"Normal pregnancy changes several liver-related laboratory values.\",\"In this patient, it is false that all liver enzymes normally decrease.\",\"Alkaline phosphatase rises because of placental production, albumin decreases due to hemodilution, and bilirubin is generally unchanged. Aminotransferases such as AST and ALT do not normally decrease as a group; abnormal elevations should be evaluated.\"],\"tk\":\"Know normal liver test changes in pregnancy.\",\"pr\":\"Do not dismiss elevated AST or ALT as normal pregnancy physiology.\",\"tp\":\"Compare pregnancy liver tests with pregnancy-specific normal changes.\",\"cn\":\"In normal pregnancy alkaline phosphatase (ALP) rises 2-4-fold from the placenta while albumin falls and bilirubin and transaminases do not rise.\",\"sl\":\"Serum albumin falls 20-40% (dilution); alkaline phosphatase (ALP) increases (doubled) due to placental production; alanine aminotransferase (ALT) and aspartate aminotransferase (AST) fall, so mild elevation may be significant; bilirubin does not change.\",\"s\":[[\"L04_0\",[2]],[\"L22_0\",[45]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M66\"},{\"id\":\"M67\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":4,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"PUQE score\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which score is used to quantify severity of nausea and vomiting in pregnancy and hyperemesis gravidarum?\",\"o\":[\"International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO) gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (GTN) score\",\"Bishop score\",\"Apgar score\",\"PUQE score\",\"CURB-65 score\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"The International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO)/World Health Organization (WHO) gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (GTN) score stratifies gestational trophoblastic neoplasia into low- and high-risk groups.\",\"The Bishop score assesses cervical favorability for induction using dilation, effacement, station, consistency, and position.\",\"The Apgar score assesses neonatal adaptation after birth using appearance, pulse, grimace, activity, and respiration.\",\"PUQE is the score for nausea and vomiting severity in pregnancy. The Pregnancy-Unique Quantification of Emesis and Nausea score, or PUQE score, assesses nausea duration, vomiting episodes, and retching episodes.\",\"CURB-65 estimates severity of community-acquired pneumonia and is unrelated to hyperemesis severity.\"],\"e\":[\"The Pregnancy-Unique Quantification of Emesis and Nausea score, or PUQE score, assesses nausea duration, vomiting episodes, and retching episodes.\",\"In this patient, pUQE is the score for nausea and vomiting severity in pregnancy.\",\"It helps quantify symptom severity in nausea and vomiting of pregnancy and hyperemesis gravidarum. Assessment also includes hydration, ketones, electrolytes, weight loss, and exclusion of alternative diagnoses.\"],\"tk\":\"Identify scoring tools used in common pregnancy conditions.\",\"pr\":\"PUQE is for vomiting; Bishop is for cervix.\",\"tp\":\"Use severity scoring plus hydration and electrolytes to guide hyperemesis management.\",\"cn\":\"The Pregnancy-Unique Quantification of Emesis (PUQE) score grades the severity of nausea and vomiting in pregnancy.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M67\"},{\"id\":\"M68\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":4,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"First-line hyperemesis antiemetic\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which medication is an appropriate first-line antiemetic for hyperemesis gravidarum?\",\"o\":[\"Methotrexate\",\"Warfarin\",\"Misoprostol\",\"Ondansetron as routine first-line sole therapy\",\"Cyclizine\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Methotrexate is used for selected ectopic pregnancies and low-risk gestational trophoblastic neoplasia, not for routine miscarriage or urinary symptoms.\",\"Warfarin is an anticoagulant and is not an antiemetic. Clinical physiology is decisive.\",\"Misoprostol is used for selected medical management of miscarriage, but molar pregnancy is usually evacuated by suction.\",\"Ondansetron is effective for nausea and vomiting in pregnancy but is commonly reserved after first-line antiemetics.\",\"Cyclizine is a first-line antiemetic option. Hyperemesis gravidarum is severe nausea and vomiting in pregnancy causing dehydration, ketonuria, electrolyte disturbance, or weight loss.\"],\"e\":[\"Hyperemesis gravidarum is severe nausea and vomiting in pregnancy causing dehydration, ketonuria, electrolyte disturbance, or weight loss.\",\"This presentation shows that cyclizine is a first-line antiemetic option.\",\"First-line antiemetics commonly include antihistamines such as cyclizine and phenothiazines, with escalation to other agents if needed. Thiamine should be given before dextrose-containing fluids in prolonged vomiting.\"],\"tk\":\"Choose safe initial antiemetic therapy in pregnancy.\",\"pr\":\"Cyclizine is commonly used as a first-line antihistamine antiemetic in pregnancy.\",\"tp\":\"Assess ketones, electrolytes, and hydration rather than treating nausea alone.\",\"cn\":\"Cyclizine is a first-line antiemetic for hyperemesis gravidarum; ondansetron is reserved for severe cases.\",\"sl\":\"Antiemetics: pyridoxine (vitamin B6) 10-25 mg three times a day, cyclizine 50 mg oral/intravenous/intramuscular, metoclopramide 10 mg, domperidone 10 mg, ondansetron 4-8 mg (severe cases), steroids (severe cases).\",\"s\":[[\"L04_0\",[8]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M68\"},{\"id\":\"M69\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":4,\"ty\":[\"Mx\",\"Complication\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Thiamine before dextrose in hyperemesis\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"In hyperemesis gravidarum with prolonged vomiting, what should be avoided before giving thiamine?\",\"o\":[\"Dextrose-containing intravenous fluids\",\"Normal saline\",\"Antiemetic therapy\",\"Potassium replacement when indicated\",\"Hartmann solution\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Dextrose should be avoided until thiamine has been administered. Prolonged vomiting can cause thiamine deficiency.\",\"Isotonic crystalloid is appropriate for volume resuscitation in hyperemesis, with electrolyte replacement guided by laboratory results.\",\"Antiemetic therapy is appropriate in hyperemesis and should be combined with fluid, electrolyte, and thiamine management as indicated.\",\"Potassium replacement is appropriate when vomiting has caused hypokalemia, with monitoring of renal function and serum electrolytes.\",\"Isotonic crystalloid is appropriate for volume resuscitation in hyperemesis, with electrolyte replacement guided by laboratory results.\"],\"e\":[\"Prolonged vomiting can cause thiamine deficiency.\",\"This presentation shows that dextrose should be avoided until thiamine has been administered.\",\"Giving dextrose before thiamine may precipitate Wernicke encephalopathy because carbohydrate metabolism increases thiamine demand. Patients with severe or prolonged vomiting should receive thiamine before dextrose-containing fluids.\"],\"tk\":\"Prevent Wernicke encephalopathy in hyperemesis gravidarum.\",\"pr\":\"Thiamine before dextrose is a lifesaving sequence.\",\"tp\":\"Prescribe thiamine early for prolonged vomiting or admission with hyperemesis.\",\"cn\":\"In hyperemesis, avoid dextrose-containing fluids before thiamine because they can precipitate Wernicke encephalopathy.\",\"sl\":\"Rehydrate with 0.9% saline with added potassium chloride (KCl). Dextrose-containing fluids are avoided except in women with diabetes mellitus (DM); they may precipitate Wernicke encephalopathy, which is prevented by routine thiamine.\",\"s\":[[\"L04_0\",[8]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M69\"},{\"id\":\"M70\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":4,\"ty\":[\"Complication\",\"Dx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Mallory-Weiss tear in hyperemesis\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A pregnant patient with severe vomiting develops hematemesis. What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"o\":[\"Mallory-Weiss tear\",\"Hemorrhoidal bleeding\",\"Placenta previa\",\"Ovarian torsion\",\"Twin-twin transfusion syndrome\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Hematemesis after forceful vomiting suggests a Mallory-Weiss tear. Forceful recurrent vomiting can cause a longitudinal mucosal tear at the gastroesophageal junction, known as a Mallory-Weiss tear.\",\"Hemorrhoids cause bright red rectal bleeding rather than hematemesis.\",\"Placenta previa typically causes painless antepartum bleeding and makes digital vaginal examination unsafe until placental location is defined.\",\"Ovarian torsion causes acute pelvic pain and vomiting but does not usually explain hematemesis after repeated retching.\",\"Twin-twin transfusion syndrome is a monochorionic twin complication and does not cause maternal upper gastrointestinal bleeding.\"],\"e\":[\"Forceful recurrent vomiting can cause a longitudinal mucosal tear at the gastroesophageal junction, known as a Mallory-Weiss tear.\",\"In this patient, hematemesis after forceful vomiting suggests a Mallory-Weiss tear.\",\"It presents with hematemesis after retching or vomiting. Management is usually supportive, but ongoing bleeding requires endoscopic assessment.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize gastrointestinal complications of severe vomiting in pregnancy.\",\"pr\":\"Blood after repeated retching points to a Mallory-Weiss tear.\",\"tp\":\"Stabilize first and assess the volume of bleeding before assuming it is minor.\",\"cn\":\"Haematemesis after severe vomiting in hyperemesis gravidarum suggests a Mallory-Weiss tear.\",\"sl\":\"Risks of hyperemesis gravidarum: fetal growth restriction (FGR), maternal hyponatraemia (central pontine myelinolysis), thiamine (B1) deficiency (Wernicke encephalopathy) and Mallory-Weiss tear.\",\"s\":[[\"L04_0\",[5]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M70\"},{\"id\":\"M71\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":6,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Endometrial sampling in AUB\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 42-year-old woman has persistent abnormal uterine bleeding despite medical management and endometrial thickness of 14 mm on ultrasound. What is the next appropriate step?\",\"o\":[\"Repeat ultrasound in 5 years\",\"Reassurance only\",\"Endometrial biopsy\",\"Ovarian detorsion\",\"Combined oral contraception without endometrial assessment\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Ultrasound is a first-line imaging modality for many obstetric and gynecologic conditions because it directly evaluates pelvic or intrauterine anatomy.\",\"Reassurance or observation is appropriate only when the clinical and imaging findings indicate a low-risk condition that does not require urgent intervention.\",\"AUB with thickened endometrium warrants endometrial biopsy. Abnormal uterine bleeding in patients aged 45 years or older, or younger patients with risk factors or concerning ultrasound findings, requires evaluation for endometrial hyperplasia or malignancy.\",\"Urgent ovarian detorsion is indicated when torsion is suspected to preserve ovarian perfusion.\",\"Empiric hormonal treatment without appropriate endometrial assessment can delay diagnosis when abnormal bleeding warrants tissue evaluation.\"],\"e\":[\"Abnormal uterine bleeding in patients aged 45 years or older, or younger patients with risk factors or concerning ultrasound findings, requires evaluation for endometrial hyperplasia or malignancy.\",\"In this patient, aUB with thickened endometrium warrants endometrial biopsy.\",\"A thickened endometrium in a 42-year-old with abnormal bleeding warrants endometrial sampling. Biopsy guides treatment and prevents missed premalignant disease.\"],\"tk\":\"Identify when endometrial sampling is needed in abnormal uterine bleeding.\",\"pr\":\"Endometrial thickness is meaningful only in the context of age, cycle, and bleeding pattern.\",\"tp\":\"Do not treat recurrent AUB blindly when biopsy is indicated.\",\"cn\":\"Women aged 40 or over with persistent abnormal uterine bleeding (AUB), especially after failed medical treatment, need endometrial biopsy.\",\"sl\":\"Sample the endometrium at 40+ or with risk factors for unopposed oestrogen; re-evaluate if treatment fails or malignancy is suspected. Endometrial biopsy excludes hyperplasia or cancer.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_1\",[13,17,27]],[\"L33_0\",[59]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"M71\"},{\"id\":\"M72\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":8,\"L\":36,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Mixed urinary incontinence conservative therapy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A woman has mixed urinary incontinence. What is the appropriate initial conservative treatment for the stress component?\",\"o\":[\"Pelvic floor muscle training for at least 3 months\",\"Methotrexate\",\"Immediate botulinum toxin injection\",\"Hysterectomy\",\"Bladder training for the urgency component\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Pelvic floor muscle training is first-line conservative treatment. Mixed urinary incontinence has both stress and urgency components, so treatment is guided by the predominant or most bothersome symptom.\",\"Methotrexate is used for selected ectopic pregnancies and low-risk gestational trophoblastic neoplasia, not for routine miscarriage or urinary symptoms.\",\"Intradetrusor botulinum toxin A is an escalation option for refractory overactive bladder after conservative and medication therapy.\",\"Hysterectomy is major surgery reserved for selected definitive indications and is not first-line for uncomplicated functional disease.\",\"Bladder training is first-line behavioral treatment for urgency-predominant urinary incontinence and overactive bladder.\"],\"e\":[\"Mixed urinary incontinence has both stress and urgency components, so treatment is guided by the predominant or most bothersome symptom.\",\"In this patient, pelvic floor muscle training is first-line conservative treatment.\",\"Pelvic floor muscle training for at least 3 months is first-line for the stress component and may be combined with bladder training for urgency symptoms. Conservative therapy should be optimized before procedural treatment.\"],\"tk\":\"Treat mixed incontinence by identifying and targeting the predominant component.\",\"pr\":\"Mixed incontinence often needs both pelvic floor training and bladder training.\",\"tp\":\"Ask which symptom bothers the patient most before escalating therapy.\",\"cn\":\"Pelvic floor muscle training (PFMT) for at least 3 months is first-line for stress and mixed urinary incontinence.\",\"sl\":\"National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE): pelvic floor muscle training (PFMT) of at least three months should be offered as first-line treatment to all women with stress (SUI) or mixed urinary incontinence (MUI).\",\"s\":[[\"L36_0\",[73]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":4,\"rs\":\"F007\"},{\"id\":\"M73\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":13,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Pulmonary embolism in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A pregnant patient develops sudden shortness of breath with pleuritic chest pain. What is the most concerning diagnosis?\",\"o\":[\"Hyperemesis gravidarum\",\"Amniotic fluid embolism\",\"Pelvic organ prolapse\",\"Threatened miscarriage\",\"Pulmonary embolism\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Hyperemesis gravidarum causes persistent nausea, vomiting, dehydration, and weight loss rather than sudden pleuritic chest pain.\",\"Amniotic fluid embolism usually causes abrupt hypoxemia, hypotension, and often coagulopathy around labor or the immediate postpartum period.\",\"Pelvic organ prolapse causes a vaginal bulge or pressure symptoms and does not cause acute pleuritic dyspnea.\",\"Threatened miscarriage causes early-pregnancy bleeding with a closed cervical os and an ongoing intrauterine pregnancy.\",\"Sudden dyspnea and pleuritic pain in pregnancy suggest pulmonary embolism. Pregnancy is a hypercoagulable state that increases the risk of venous thromboembolism.\"],\"e\":[\"Pregnancy is a hypercoagulable state that increases the risk of venous thromboembolism.\",\"In this patient, sudden dyspnea and pleuritic pain in pregnancy suggest pulmonary embolism.\",\"Sudden dyspnea with pleuritic chest pain should raise concern for pulmonary embolism, especially if tachycardia, hypoxia, syncope, or leg symptoms are present. Evaluation and anticoagulation decisions should proceed urgently because PE is a major cause of maternal mortality.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize symptoms of pulmonary embolism in pregnancy.\",\"pr\":\"Pleuritic chest pain in pregnancy is PE until proven otherwise.\",\"tp\":\"Check oxygen saturation and start urgent venous thromboembolism (VTE) pathway assessment.\",\"cn\":\"Sudden dyspnoea with pleuritic chest pain in pregnancy should be treated as pulmonary embolism (PE) until excluded.\",\"sl\":\"Pulmonary embolism (PE) signs and symptoms: dyspnoea (most common), palpitations or tachycardia, chest pain, haemoptysis, hypoxia or cyanosis, tachypnoea, hypotension and collapse.\",\"s\":[[\"L13_0\",[13]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M73\"},{\"id\":\"M74\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":11,\"ty\":[\"Dx\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Peripartum cardiomyopathy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A pregnant or postpartum patient has new left ventricular systolic dysfunction with reduced ejection fraction and no other clear cause. What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"o\":[\"Myocarditis\",\"Complete molar pregnancy\",\"Peripartum cardiomyopathy\",\"Ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome\",\"Stress urinary incontinence\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Myocarditis can cause new ventricular dysfunction but is usually associated with an infectious, inflammatory, or toxic context rather than the characteristic peripartum timing.\",\"A complete hydatidiform mole has diffuse trophoblastic proliferation, usually no fetal tissue, and often markedly elevated beta-hCG.\",\"New reduced ejection fraction around pregnancy suggests peripartum cardiomyopathy. Peripartum cardiomyopathy is heart failure with reduced left ventricular ejection fraction developing toward the end of pregnancy or in the months after delivery without another identifiable cause.\",\"Ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome follows fertility treatment and causes ovarian enlargement, third spacing, and hemoconcentration rather than isolated new peripartum systolic dysfunction.\",\"Stress urinary incontinence causes leakage with cough, sneeze, exertion, or other increases in intra-abdominal pressure.\"],\"e\":[\"Peripartum cardiomyopathy is heart failure with reduced left ventricular ejection fraction developing toward the end of pregnancy or in the months after delivery without another identifiable cause.\",\"In this patient, new reduced ejection fraction around pregnancy suggests peripartum cardiomyopathy.\",\"Symptoms include dyspnea, orthopnea, edema, fatigue, and reduced exercise tolerance. Management requires multidisciplinary cardio-obstetric care and pregnancy-safe heart failure therapy.\"],\"tk\":\"Identify peripartum cardiomyopathy in pregnant or postpartum patients with systolic dysfunction.\",\"pr\":\"Do not attribute postpartum dyspnea only to fatigue without considering heart failure.\",\"tp\":\"Echocardiography is central when peripartum cardiomyopathy is suspected.\",\"cn\":\"Peripartum cardiomyopathy is idiopathic left ventricular systolic dysfunction (ejection fraction below 45%) in late pregnancy or up to months postpartum.\",\"sl\":\"Peripartum cardiomyopathy is an idiopathic cardiomyopathy developing late in pregnancy or early postpartum (up to 6 months) without another identifiable cause, characterised by left ventricular systolic dysfunction with ejection fraction (EF) below 45%; it tends to recur.\",\"s\":[[\"L11_0\",[30]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M74\"},{\"id\":\"M75\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":4,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Intrahepatic cholestasis presentation\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A pregnant patient has pruritus of the palms and soles with elevated liver enzymes. What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"o\":[\"Incomplete miscarriage\",\"Polymorphic eruption of pregnancy\",\"Ovarian torsion\",\"Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy\",\"Placenta previa\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Complete miscarriage means all products of conception have passed and the uterus is empty.\",\"Polymorphic eruption of pregnancy causes a pruritic rash, usually beginning in abdominal striae, rather than isolated palm-and-sole pruritus with liver-test abnormalities.\",\"Ovarian torsion causes acute pelvic pain and vomiting and does not produce cholestatic pruritus.\",\"Palmar and plantar pruritus with abnormal liver tests suggests obstetric cholestasis. Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy typically presents in the second or third trimester with pruritus, especially of the palms and soles, without a primary rash.\",\"Placenta previa typically causes painless antepartum bleeding and makes digital vaginal examination unsafe until placental location is defined.\"],\"e\":[\"Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy typically presents in the second or third trimester with pruritus, especially of the palms and soles, without a primary rash.\",\"In this patient, palmar and plantar pruritus with abnormal liver tests suggests obstetric cholestasis.\",\"Serum bile acids are elevated and transaminases may also rise. The condition is important because fetal risks are related to bile acid level.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize the presentation of intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy.\",\"pr\":\"Itching of palms and soles in late pregnancy should prompt bile acid testing.\",\"tp\":\"Look for excoriations but not a primary rash in obstetric cholestasis.\",\"cn\":\"Pruritus of the palms and soles without rash and abnormal liver function tests (LFTs) in pregnancy indicate intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy.\",\"sl\":\"Obstetric cholestasis is a pregnancy-specific liver disease with pruritus affecting the whole body but particularly palms and soles, and abnormal liver function tests (LFTs); there is no rash other than excoriations.\",\"s\":[[\"L04_0\",[16,17]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M75\"},{\"id\":\"M76\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":7,\"L\":38,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Endometrioma surgery\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A patient has a 4 cm ovarian endometrioma and severe dysmenorrhea despite medical therapy. What surgical management is appropriate?\",\"o\":[\"Ultrasound-guided aspiration of the endometrioma alone\",\"Intravesical botulinum toxin\",\"Uterine artery embolization as first-line treatment\",\"Emergency cesarean delivery\",\"Laparoscopic cystectomy with excision of the endometrioma wall\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Ultrasound is a first-line imaging modality for many obstetric and gynecologic conditions because it directly evaluates pelvic or intrauterine anatomy.\",\"Intradetrusor botulinum toxin A is an escalation option for refractory overactive bladder after conservative and medication therapy.\",\"Uterine artery embolization is used mainly for fibroid-related bleeding and is not first-line surgery for an ovarian endometrioma.\",\"Cesarean delivery is used when vaginal birth is unsafe or urgent birth is required; maternal stabilization still precedes operative delivery when feasible.\",\"Symptomatic 4 cm endometrioma can be treated with laparoscopic cystectomy/excision. Endometrioma is ovarian endometriosis and may present with dysmenorrhea, chronic pelvic pain, dyspareunia, or infertility.\"],\"e\":[\"Endometrioma is ovarian endometriosis and may present with dysmenorrhea, chronic pelvic pain, dyspareunia, or infertility.\",\"In this patient, symptomatic 4 cm endometrioma can be treated with laparoscopic cystectomy/excision.\",\"Symptomatic endometriomas around 4 cm that fail medical therapy can be managed surgically, typically by laparoscopic cystectomy or excision of the cyst wall rather than simple drainage alone. Ovarian reserve and fertility goals should be discussed before surgery.\"],\"tk\":\"Choose surgical management for symptomatic endometrioma.\",\"pr\":\"Drainage alone has a higher recurrence risk than cyst wall excision.\",\"tp\":\"Discuss ovarian reserve before endometrioma surgery.\",\"cn\":\"Symptomatic endometriomas are treated by laparoscopic cystectomy with excision of the cyst wall rather than drainage or ablation.\",\"sl\":\"Endometrioma should be deroofed and removed instead of ablated laparoscopically; offer laparoscopic ovarian cystectomy with excision of the cyst wall, considering ovarian reserve. Endometriomas should be removed if over 5 cm, symptomatic or affecting fertility.\",\"s\":[[\"L38_0\",[36,44]],[\"L40_0\",[18]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M76\"},{\"id\":\"M77\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":6,\"L\":29,\"ty\":[\"Dx\",\"Cause\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Functional hypothalamic amenorrhea\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A patient develops secondary amenorrhea after marked psychological stress and intense exercise. What is the most likely cause?\",\"o\":[\"Androgen-secreting ovarian tumor\",\"Functional hypothalamic amenorrhea\",\"Polycystic ovary syndrome\",\"Complete molar pregnancy\",\"Asherman syndrome\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"An androgen-secreting ovarian tumor usually causes rapid virilization or marked hyperandrogenism, not amenorrhea after stress and intense exercise alone.\",\"Stress and intense exercise point to hypothalamic suppression. Functional hypothalamic amenorrhea results from reduced pulsatile gonadotrophin-releasing hormone (GnRH) secretion due to stress, weight loss, undernutrition, or excessive exercise.\",\"Polycystic ovary syndrome causes chronic anovulation with hyperandrogenic features rather than amenorrhea driven by acute stress and intense exercise alone.\",\"A complete hydatidiform mole has diffuse trophoblastic proliferation, usually no fetal tissue, and often markedly elevated beta-hCG.\",\"Asherman syndrome causes amenorrhea from intrauterine adhesions, often after uterine instrumentation or infection.\"],\"e\":[\"Functional hypothalamic amenorrhea results from reduced pulsatile GnRH secretion due to stress, weight loss, undernutrition, or excessive exercise.\",\"In this patient, stress and intense exercise point to hypothalamic suppression.\",\"This leads to low gonadotropins and low estradiol with anovulation. Management focuses on correcting energy deficit, reducing stress, and protecting bone health.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize functional hypothalamic amenorrhea as a cause of secondary amenorrhea.\",\"pr\":\"Exercise plus stress can switch off the reproductive axis.\",\"tp\":\"Ask about diet, weight change, exercise, and stress in secondary amenorrhea.\",\"cn\":\"Secondary amenorrhea after stress, weight loss or intense exercise is functional hypothalamic amenorrhea.\",\"sl\":\"Hypothalamic amenorrhoea: normal or low follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and luteinizing hormone (LH); causes include some medications and extremes of weight loss, stress or exercise.\",\"s\":[[\"L29_0\",[16]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F449\"},{\"id\":\"M78\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":7,\"L\":32,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Male-factor infertility testing\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A couple has been trying to conceive for 3 years. What is the first investigation to assess the male partner?\",\"o\":[\"Hysterosalpingography of the female partner\",\"Semen fluid analysis\",\"Endometrial biopsy\",\"Serum cancer antigen 125 (CA-125)\",\"Sperm DNA fragmentation testing as the first investigation\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Hysterosalpingography evaluates tubal patency in infertility and is not used to assess retained postpartum tissue.\",\"Semen analysis is the first-line male-factor investigation. Infertility is failure to conceive after 12 months of regular unprotected intercourse, or earlier evaluation in selected older patients or those with risk factors.\",\"Endometrial biopsy samples the uterine lining for hyperplasia or malignancy when age, risk factors, or persistent abnormal bleeding warrant sampling.\",\"Cancer antigen 125 (CA-125) can support ovarian-mass risk assessment, particularly after menopause, but is not a stand-alone diagnostic test.\",\"Sperm DNA fragmentation testing is not a routine first-line investigation before standard semen analysis.\"],\"e\":[\"Infertility is failure to conceive after 12 months of regular unprotected intercourse, or earlier evaluation in selected older patients or those with risk factors.\",\"This presentation shows that semen analysis is the first-line male-factor investigation.\",\"Semen analysis is the initial test for male-factor infertility because it is noninvasive and assesses sperm concentration, motility, and morphology. Both partners should be evaluated in parallel.\"],\"tk\":\"Begin infertility evaluation with simple tests for both partners.\",\"pr\":\"Male-factor infertility is common and should be checked early.\",\"tp\":\"Do not complete an extensive female workup before ordering a semen analysis.\",\"cn\":\"Semen analysis is the first investigation of the male partner in infertility.\",\"sl\":\"Basic work-up for infertility includes detailed history and examination and semen analysis (World Health Organization [WHO] 2010 reference values).\",\"s\":[[\"L32_0\",[12,13]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M78\"},{\"id\":\"M79\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":6,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"AUB with enlarged uterus\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 46-year-old woman has heavy menstrual bleeding, dysmenorrhea, an irregularly enlarged uterus, and endometrial thickness of 14 mm. What is the next appropriate step?\",\"o\":[\"Bladder training\",\"Reassurance only\",\"Repeat ultrasound after menopause\",\"Antenatal corticosteroids\",\"Endometrial biopsy\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Bladder training is first-line behavioral treatment for urgency-predominant urinary incontinence and overactive bladder.\",\"Reassurance or observation is appropriate only when the clinical and imaging findings indicate a low-risk condition that does not require urgent intervention.\",\"Ultrasound is a first-line imaging modality for many obstetric and gynecologic conditions because it directly evaluates pelvic or intrauterine anatomy.\",\"Antenatal corticosteroids are used to reduce neonatal complications of preterm birth and have no role in evaluating nonpregnant abnormal uterine bleeding.\",\"Abnormal uterine bleeding (AUB) with endometrial thickness of 14 mm warrants biopsy. Heavy menstrual bleeding with a thickened endometrium requires evaluation for endometrial hyperplasia or malignancy, especially when bleeding is abnormal or risk factors are present.\"],\"e\":[\"Heavy menstrual bleeding with a thickened endometrium requires evaluation for endometrial hyperplasia or malignancy, especially when bleeding is abnormal or risk factors are present.\",\"In this patient, aUB with endometrial thickness of 14 mm warrants biopsy.\",\"An irregularly enlarged uterus suggests fibroids, but structural pathology does not exclude coexisting endometrial disease. Endometrial biopsy is appropriate before definitive treatment of AUB when sampling criteria are met.\"],\"tk\":\"Do not miss endometrial pathology in AUB even when fibroids are suspected.\",\"pr\":\"Fibroids can explain bleeding, but biopsy may still be indicated.\",\"tp\":\"Sample the endometrium before definitive AUB treatment when risk or thickening is present.\",\"cn\":\"Women aged 40 or over with heavy menstrual bleeding (HMB) need endometrial sampling even when fibroids or adenomyosis are suspected.\",\"sl\":\"Sample the endometrium at 40+ or with risk factors for unopposed oestrogen. Investigations include transvaginal ultrasound and endometrial biopsy to exclude hyperplasia or cancer.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_1\",[13,27]],[\"L33_0\",[59]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"M71\"},{\"id\":\"M80\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":6,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"PALM-COEIN structural causes\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"In the PALM-COEIN classification (polyp, adenomyosis, leiomyoma, malignancy and hyperplasia, coagulopathy, ovulatory dysfunction, endometrial, iatrogenic, not yet classified), leiomyoma-related abnormal uterine bleeding belongs to which broad category?\",\"o\":[\"Structural causes\",\"Ovulatory dysfunction only\",\"Coagulopathy only\",\"Iatrogenic causes only\",\"Nonstructural causes\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Leiomyoma is part of the structural PALM group. The PALM-COEIN system (polyp, adenomyosis, leiomyoma, malignancy and hyperplasia, coagulopathy, ovulatory dysfunction, endometrial, iatrogenic, not yet classified) classifies abnormal uterine bleeding into structural and nonstructural causes.\",\"Ovulatory dysfunction is a COEIN etiology of abnormal uterine bleeding.\",\"Coagulopathy is one member of the nonstructural COEIN group, not the broad category containing leiomyoma.\",\"Iatrogenic causes are nonstructural COEIN causes of abnormal uterine bleeding.\",\"COEIN etiologies include coagulopathy, ovulatory dysfunction, endometrial dysfunction, iatrogenic causes, and unclassified entities; leiomyoma belongs to PALM.\"],\"e\":[\"PALM-COEIN classifies abnormal uterine bleeding into structural and nonstructural causes.\",\"This presentation shows that leiomyoma is part of the structural PALM group.\",\"PALM includes polyp, adenomyosis, leiomyoma, and malignancy/hyperplasia, which are structural causes identifiable by imaging or histology. COEIN includes coagulopathy, ovulatory dysfunction, endometrial, iatrogenic, and not otherwise classified causes.\"],\"tk\":\"Use PALM-COEIN to organize causes of abnormal uterine bleeding.\",\"pr\":\"PALM is structural; COEIN is nonstructural.\",\"tp\":\"Classifying AUB helps choose imaging, biopsy, and treatment.\",\"cn\":\"Leiomyoma is a structural (PALM) cause of abnormal uterine bleeding (AUB) in the International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO) classification.\",\"sl\":\"Structural causes of abnormal uterine bleeding (AUB): polyp, adenomyosis, leiomyoma, malignancy and hyperplasia (PALM).\",\"s\":[[\"L33_0\",[46]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M80\"},{\"id\":\"M81\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":6,\"L\":38,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Adenomyosis uterine examination\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which uterine examination finding favors adenomyosis rather than leiomyoma?\",\"o\":[\"Isolated cervical polyp\",\"Normal-sized mobile uterus\",\"Pedunculated subserosal mass\",\"Uniformly enlarged regular globular uterus\",\"Irregular nodular uterine contour\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A cervical polyp is a focal cervical lesion that may cause intermenstrual or postcoital bleeding without diffuse uterine enlargement.\",\"A normal-sized mobile uterus lacks the enlargement expected with substantial adenomyosis or leiomyoma.\",\"Leiomyomas more often produce an irregular, nodular uterine contour or a discrete fibroid mass.\",\"Adenomyosis classically causes a regular globular uterine enlargement. Adenomyosis is ectopic endometrial glands and stroma within the myometrium, causing dysmenorrhea, heavy bleeding, and a diffusely enlarged tender uterus.\",\"Leiomyomas more often produce an irregular, nodular uterine contour or a discrete fibroid mass.\"],\"e\":[\"Adenomyosis is ectopic endometrial glands and stroma within the myometrium, causing dysmenorrhea, heavy bleeding, and a diffusely enlarged tender uterus.\",\"In this patient, adenomyosis classically causes a regular globular uterine enlargement.\",\"The uterus is often uniformly enlarged, regular, and globular. Leiomyomas more often produce an irregularly enlarged nodular uterus depending on number and location.\"],\"tk\":\"Differentiate adenomyosis from fibroids by the pattern of uterine enlargement.\",\"pr\":\"Adenomyosis is usually smooth and globular; fibroids are often irregular.\",\"tp\":\"Ask about dysmenorrhea and examine uterine contour when distinguishing adenomyosis from fibroids.\",\"cn\":\"Adenomyosis gives a uniformly (symmetrically) enlarged, globular, often tender uterus; fibroids give an irregular nodular uterus.\",\"sl\":\"Adenomyosis presents with heavy menstrual bleeding, progressive dysmenorrhoea and deep dyspareunia; examination reveals a symmetrically enlarged uterus that may be tender.\",\"s\":[[\"L38_0\",[48]],[\"L33_0\",[55]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M81\"},{\"id\":\"R01\",\"k\":\"R\",\"b\":\"hayat\",\"g\":null,\"L\":29,\"ty\":[],\"lv\":null,\"ti\":\"Primary amenorrhoea\",\"st\":\"Primary amenorrhoea\",\"c\":\"A phenotypically female adolescent presents with primary amenorrhoea. Height 165 cm, weight on the 50th centile. Breast development is Tanner stage 4. There is no axillary or pubic hair. Imaging shows no uterus.\",\"q\":\"What is the most likely karyotype and hormonal profile?\",\"o\":[\"46,XX with normal female-range testosterone\",\"46,XY with high (male-range) testosterone\",\"46,XY with low testosterone and high follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH)\",\"45,X with low oestradiol and high FSH\",\"46,XX with high testosterone and raised 17-hydroxyprogesterone\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A 46,XX girl with Müllerian agenesis (MRKH) has normal pubic hair and normal female testosterone; the absent sexual hair here does not fit.\",\"Correct. In androgen insensitivity syndrome (AIS) the testes make male-range testosterone, but the androgen receptor does not respond, so there is no sexual hair; testosterone is aromatised to oestrogen, which gives good breast development; anti-Müllerian hormone from the testes removes the uterus.\",\"Low testosterone with high follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) is Swyer syndrome (46,XY pure gonadal dysgenesis): streak gonads, so poor breast development, and a uterus is present.\",\"Turner syndrome (45,X) gives short stature, absent breast development and a uterus present; this girl is 165 cm with Tanner 4 breasts.\",\"This is congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH), which causes virilisation (hirsutism, clitoromegaly), not absent hair, and the uterus is present.\"],\"e\":[\"Breasts present + uterus absent + no pubic or axillary hair = androgen insensitivity syndrome (AIS) until proven otherwise.\",\"Breasts develop because testosterone is aromatised to oestradiol.\",\"The uterus is absent because the testes secrete anti-Müllerian hormone (AMH).\",\"Pubic and axillary hair are absent because the androgen receptor does not work.\",\"Compare with Müllerian agenesis (MRKH): 46,XX, normal hair, normal testosterone.\"],\"tk\":\"\",\"pr\":\"\",\"tp\":\"\",\"cn\":\"Androgen insensitivity syndrome (AIS): 46,XY, male-range testosterone, breasts present, no sexual hair, no uterus.\",\"sl\":\"Normal breast development and high FSH: karyotype and pelvic imaging. Complete androgen insensitivity (CAIS): 46,XY, uterus absent, absent pubic and axillary hair (androgen dependent) and a short vagina.\",\"s\":[[\"L29_0\",[7]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"R01\"},{\"id\":\"R02\",\"k\":\"R\",\"b\":\"hayat\",\"g\":null,\"L\":29,\"ty\":[],\"lv\":null,\"ti\":\"Primary amenorrhoea\",\"st\":\"Primary amenorrhoea\",\"c\":\"A phenotypically female adolescent presents with primary amenorrhoea. Height 165 cm, weight on the 50th centile. Breast development is Tanner stage 4. There is no axillary or pubic hair. Imaging shows no uterus.\",\"q\":\"Which intervention matters most for her long-term outcome?\",\"o\":[\"Creation of a neovagina\",\"Gonadectomy\",\"Cyclical oestrogen–progestogen therapy to induce periods\",\"Uterine transplantation\",\"Gonadotrophin-releasing hormone (GnRH) agonist therapy\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Vaginal dilators (first line) or surgery help intercourse, but they do not affect her long-term health.\",\"Correct. The intra-abdominal testes carry a risk of germ-cell malignancy (gonadoblastoma, dysgerminoma), so gonadectomy is the key intervention, usually after puberty is complete, followed by oestrogen replacement.\",\"She has no uterus, so periods cannot be induced; after gonadectomy she needs oestrogen alone.\",\"Uterine transplantation is experimental and not the priority; it does not address the malignancy risk.\",\"Gonadotrophin-releasing hormone (GnRH) agonists suppress puberty; they have no role here.\"],\"e\":[\"The undescended testes in androgen insensitivity syndrome (AIS) can become malignant, so they are removed.\",\"In complete AIS gonadectomy is usually delayed until after puberty so that the girl completes breast development from her own hormones.\",\"After gonadectomy, oestrogen-only hormone replacement therapy (HRT) protects bone; no progestogen is needed because there is no uterus.\",\"The short vagina is treated with dilators first.\"],\"tk\":\"\",\"pr\":\"\",\"tp\":\"\",\"cn\":\"In androgen insensitivity syndrome, gonadectomy (after puberty) prevents gonadal malignancy; then give oestrogen replacement.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"R02\"},{\"id\":\"R03\",\"k\":\"R\",\"b\":\"hayat\",\"g\":null,\"L\":29,\"ty\":[],\"lv\":null,\"ti\":\"Primary amenorrhoea\",\"st\":\"Primary amenorrhoea\",\"c\":\"A phenotypically female adolescent presents with primary amenorrhoea. Height 165 cm, weight on the 50th centile. Breast development is Tanner stage 4. There is no axillary or pubic hair. Imaging shows no uterus.\",\"q\":\"What is the diagnosis?\",\"o\":[\"Swyer syndrome\",\"Mayer–Rokitansky–Küster–Hauser (MRKH) syndrome\",\"Androgen insensitivity syndrome (AIS)\",\"Turner syndrome\",\"5-alpha-reductase deficiency\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Swyer syndrome (46,XY gonadal dysgenesis) has streak gonads, no breast development and a uterus.\",\"Mayer–Rokitansky–Küster–Hauser syndrome (MRKH) has no uterus, but pubic and axillary hair are normal because the girl is 46,XX with normal ovaries.\",\"Correct. Normal breasts, no uterus and absent sexual hair is the classic picture of complete androgen insensitivity.\",\"Turner syndrome: short stature, poor breast development, uterus present.\",\"5-alpha-reductase deficiency presents with ambiguous genitalia and virilisation at puberty, not a female phenotype with good breasts.\"],\"e\":[\"Use two questions to sort primary amenorrhoea: are breasts present (oestrogen), and is the uterus present?\",\"Breasts present, uterus absent: MRKH (hair normal) or AIS (hair absent).\",\"Breasts absent, uterus present: Turner, Swyer or hypothalamic causes.\"],\"tk\":\"\",\"pr\":\"\",\"tp\":\"\",\"cn\":\"Breasts present, uterus absent, sexual hair absent = androgen insensitivity syndrome (AIS).\",\"sl\":\"Complete androgen insensitivity (CAIS): 46,XY, uterus absent, absent pubic and axillary hair; Mayer-Rokitansky-Kuster-Hauser syndrome: absent uterus with normal follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH).\",\"s\":[[\"L29_0\",[7]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"R03\"},{\"id\":\"R04\",\"k\":\"R\",\"b\":\"hayat\",\"g\":null,\"L\":29,\"ty\":[],\"lv\":null,\"ti\":\"Primary amenorrhoea\",\"st\":\"Primary amenorrhoea\",\"c\":\"A phenotypically female adolescent presents with primary amenorrhoea. Height 165 cm, weight on the 50th centile. Breast development is Tanner stage 4. There is no axillary or pubic hair. Imaging shows no uterus.\",\"q\":\"Which investigations confirm the diagnosis?\",\"o\":[\"Karyotype and serum testosterone\",\"Serum follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and luteinising hormone (LH) only\",\"Pelvic magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) only\",\"Anti-Müllerian hormone (AMH) and oestradiol\",\"Prolactin and thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH)\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Correct. A 46,XY karyotype with male-range testosterone confirms androgen insensitivity and separates it from MRKH (46,XX, female testosterone).\",\"Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and luteinising hormone (LH) may be normal or raised and do not separate AIS from MRKH.\",\"Imaging shows the absent uterus and may locate gonads, but it cannot confirm the cause.\",\"These do not establish the genetic sex or androgen level.\",\"Prolactin and thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) are part of the work-up of secondary amenorrhoea with a uterus; they do not help here.\"],\"e\":[\"When the uterus is absent, the next step is karyotype and testosterone.\",\"46,XY + male testosterone = AIS.\",\"46,XX + female testosterone = Müllerian agenesis (MRKH).\"],\"tk\":\"\",\"pr\":\"\",\"tp\":\"\",\"cn\":\"Karyotype + testosterone separate AIS (46,XY, high testosterone) from MRKH (46,XX, normal testosterone).\",\"sl\":\"Karyotype and pelvic imaging differentiate premature ovarian failure (46,XX, uterus present) from complete androgen insensitivity (46,XY, uterus absent).\",\"s\":[[\"L29_0\",[5,6,7]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"R04\"},{\"id\":\"R05\",\"k\":\"R\",\"b\":\"hayat\",\"g\":null,\"L\":8,\"ty\":[],\"lv\":null,\"ti\":\"Antepartum haemorrhage\",\"st\":\"Antepartum haemorrhage\",\"c\":\"A woman at 30 weeks' gestation presents with vaginal bleeding. An earlier ultrasound showed that the placenta is not praevia.\",\"q\":\"After assessing maternal and fetal condition, which examination is appropriate next?\",\"o\":[\"Digital vaginal examination is contraindicated, so no vaginal examination at all\",\"Speculum examination\",\"Repeat transabdominal ultrasound before any vaginal examination\",\"Immediate caesarean section\",\"Amniotomy\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Avoiding all vaginal examination applies when placenta praevia has not been excluded; here it has been excluded.\",\"Correct. With placenta praevia excluded, a speculum examination is safe and shows the source (cervical or vaginal causes), the amount of bleeding and whether the cervix is dilating.\",\"Ultrasound is needed when the placental site is unknown; here it is already known, so it does not need to come before the speculum.\",\"Delivery is indicated for maternal or fetal compromise, which is not described.\",\"Amniotomy has no role at 30 weeks in an undiagnosed bleed.\"],\"e\":[\"In every APH, ask first whether placenta praevia has been excluded.\",\"If not: ultrasound before any vaginal examination, and never a digital examination.\",\"If praevia is excluded: speculum examination for local causes (ectropion, polyp, infection, carcinoma) and cervical dilatation.\"],\"tk\":\"\",\"pr\":\"\",\"tp\":\"\",\"cn\":\"Antepartum haemorrhage (APH): if placenta praevia is excluded, speculum examination looks for local causes and cervical change.\",\"sl\":\"Speculum examination can be useful to identify cervical dilatation or visualise a lower genital tract cause for the APH; a clinically suspicious cervix is referred for colposcopy.\",\"s\":[[\"L08_0\",[14]]],\"f\":\"r\",\"n\":\"The student recalled the question but not the answer options, so the options were rebuilt. Check Dr. Hadeel's APH slides.\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"R05\"},{\"id\":\"R06\",\"k\":\"R\",\"b\":\"hayat\",\"g\":null,\"L\":8,\"ty\":[],\"lv\":null,\"ti\":\"Antepartum haemorrhage\",\"st\":\"Antepartum haemorrhage\",\"c\":\"A woman at 30 weeks' gestation presents with vaginal bleeding. An earlier ultrasound showed that the placenta is not praevia.\",\"q\":\"She is rhesus (Rh) D-negative. What is the correct anti-D plan?\",\"o\":[\"Wait for the Kleihauer result, then decide whether to give anti-D\",\"Give anti-D immunoglobulin and take a Kleihauer test to see whether more is needed\",\"No anti-D is needed before 34 weeks\",\"Give anti-D only if the baby is born RhD-positive\",\"Give anti-D only if bleeding recurs\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Do not wait: anti-D should be given within 72 hours of the sensitising event, and the Kleihauer result only decides whether extra doses are needed.\",\"Correct. In an RhD-negative woman with APH after 20 weeks, give anti-D (at least 500 IU) and send a Kleihauer test to measure the fetomaternal haemorrhage; give more anti-D if the bleed is larger.\",\"Anti-D is given for any sensitising event from 12 weeks onwards (and from 20 weeks with a Kleihauer test).\",\"Postnatal anti-D is a separate dose; an antenatal sensitising event needs its own dose.\",\"Each sensitising event needs anti-D; recurrent bleeding needs repeat doses, not the first one.\"],\"e\":[\"Kleihauer–Betke quantifies fetal red cells in maternal blood.\",\"Anti-D is given without waiting for the result; the result decides if more is needed.\",\"Give within 72 hours of the event.\"],\"tk\":\"\",\"pr\":\"\",\"tp\":\"\",\"cn\":\"RhD-negative with APH after 20 weeks: give anti-D within 72 hours plus a Kleihauer test to quantify fetomaternal haemorrhage.\",\"sl\":\"The Kleihauer test should be performed in rhesus D (RhD)-negative women to quantify fetomaternal haemorrhage (FMH) in order to gauge the dose of anti-D immunoglobulin required.\",\"s\":[[\"L08_0\",[18,23]]],\"f\":\"r\",\"n\":\"The student did not record the answer, so the options were rebuilt. Check Dr. Hadeel's antepartum haemorrhage (APH) slides.\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"R06\"},{\"id\":\"R07\",\"k\":\"R\",\"b\":\"hayat\",\"g\":null,\"L\":9,\"ty\":[],\"lv\":null,\"ti\":\"Postpartum haemorrhage\",\"st\":\"Postpartum haemorrhage\",\"c\":\"A woman has a postpartum haemorrhage (PPH) with an estimated blood loss of about 1000 mL. Platelets 100 × 10⁹/L, fibrinogen 1.4 g/L.\",\"q\":\"Which blood product should she receive?\",\"o\":[\"Platelet transfusion\",\"Fresh frozen plasma (FFP) alone\",\"Cryoprecipitate\",\"Albumin\",\"No blood product; give tranexamic acid only\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Platelets are given when the count falls below 75 × 10⁹/L in ongoing PPH; 100 is above that.\",\"Fresh frozen plasma (FFP) replaces clotting factors, but a fibrinogen of 1.4 g/L is best corrected with a concentrated source of fibrinogen.\",\"Correct. Fibrinogen below 2 g/L with ongoing PPH should be corrected with cryoprecipitate (or fibrinogen concentrate).\",\"Albumin is a colloid, not a blood product for coagulopathy.\",\"Tranexamic acid helps, but low fibrinogen still needs replacing.\"],\"e\":[\"Fibrinogen falls early in PPH and predicts severity.\",\"Targets in ongoing PPH: fibrinogen above 2 g/L, platelets above 75 × 10⁹/L, haemoglobin above 80 g/L.\",\"Cryoprecipitate is the fibrinogen-rich product.\"],\"tk\":\"\",\"pr\":\"\",\"tp\":\"\",\"cn\":\"In postpartum haemorrhage (PPH), fibrinogen below 2 g/L needs cryoprecipitate; platelets are given below 75 × 10⁹/L.\",\"sl\":\"Platelets if haemorrhage is ongoing and platelet count less than 75 × 10⁹/L; 2 pools of cryoprecipitate if haemorrhage is ongoing and fibrinogen less than 2 g/L.\",\"s\":[[\"L09_0\",[11]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"R07\"},{\"id\":\"R08\",\"k\":\"R\",\"b\":\"hayat\",\"g\":null,\"L\":9,\"ty\":[],\"lv\":null,\"ti\":\"Postpartum haemorrhage\",\"st\":\"Postpartum haemorrhage\",\"c\":\"A woman has a postpartum haemorrhage (PPH) with an estimated blood loss of about 1000 mL. Platelets 100 × 10⁹/L, fibrinogen 1.4 g/L.\",\"q\":\"What is the first step in management?\",\"o\":[\"Bimanual uterine compression\",\"Resuscitation: call for help, ABC, two large-bore cannulas, fluids and blood\",\"Intrauterine balloon tamponade\",\"Carboprost injection\",\"Exploration under anaesthesia\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Uterine massage and compression follow quickly, but the first step is calling for help and resuscitation.\",\"Correct. Management of PPH starts with communication and resuscitation (call for help, airway, breathing, circulation, two 14–16G cannulas, bloods, warm fluids), done at the same time as finding and treating the cause.\",\"Balloon tamponade comes after medical treatment fails.\",\"Uterotonics are given after resuscitation has started and oxytocin has been tried.\",\"Theatre comes after initial measures, or earlier if there is trauma or retained tissue.\"],\"e\":[\"The four parallel components of PPH management are communication, resuscitation, monitoring and stopping the bleeding.\",\"Causes: Tone, Trauma, Tissue, Thrombin.\"],\"tk\":\"\",\"pr\":\"\",\"tp\":\"\",\"cn\":\"Postpartum haemorrhage (PPH): call for help and resuscitate first (ABC, two large-bore lines), then treat the cause (4 Ts).\",\"sl\":\"Resuscitation: call for help, airway, breathing, circulation, two large-bore cannulas, warmed fluids and blood.\",\"s\":[[\"L09_0\",[13]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"R08\"},{\"id\":\"R09\",\"k\":\"R\",\"b\":\"hayat\",\"g\":null,\"L\":9,\"ty\":[],\"lv\":null,\"ti\":\"Postpartum haemorrhage\",\"st\":\"Postpartum haemorrhage\",\"c\":\"A woman has a postpartum haemorrhage (PPH) with an estimated blood loss of about 1000 mL. Platelets 100 × 10⁹/L, fibrinogen 1.4 g/L.\",\"q\":\"Medical treatment has failed. What is the first-line surgical measure?\",\"o\":[\"B-Lynch compression suture\",\"Bilateral uterine artery ligation\",\"Intrauterine balloon tamponade\",\"Hysterectomy\",\"Internal iliac artery ligation\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A compression suture needs a laparotomy; it comes after balloon tamponade fails.\",\"Vessel ligation is a later step.\",\"Correct. Intrauterine balloon tamponade is the first-line 'surgical' step for uterine atony when drugs fail.\",\"Hysterectomy is the last resort, though it should not be delayed too long.\",\"Internal iliac ligation is a later step done by experienced surgeons.\"],\"e\":[\"Escalation after uterotonics: balloon tamponade, then B-Lynch suture, then uterine or internal iliac artery ligation or interventional radiology, then hysterectomy.\"],\"tk\":\"\",\"pr\":\"\",\"tp\":\"\",\"cn\":\"Atonic postpartum haemorrhage (PPH) not responding to drugs: intrauterine balloon tamponade first, then compression sutures, ligation, then hysterectomy.\",\"sl\":\"If pharmacological measures fail: intrauterine balloon tamponade is the first-line surgical intervention.\",\"s\":[[\"L09_0\",[16]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"R09\"},{\"id\":\"R10\",\"k\":\"R\",\"b\":\"hayat\",\"g\":null,\"L\":9,\"ty\":[],\"lv\":null,\"ti\":\"Postpartum haemorrhage\",\"st\":\"Postpartum haemorrhage\",\"c\":\"A woman has a postpartum haemorrhage (PPH) with an estimated blood loss of about 1000 mL. Platelets 100 × 10⁹/L, fibrinogen 1.4 g/L.\",\"q\":\"She has asthma and pre-eclampsia, and oxytocin has not controlled the atony. Which uterotonic should she receive?\",\"o\":[\"Ergometrine\",\"Syntometrine (oxytocin + ergometrine)\",\"Carboprost\",\"Misoprostol 800 micrograms sublingually\",\"Dinoprostone pessary\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Ergometrine causes vasoconstriction and hypertension, so it is contraindicated in pre-eclampsia.\",\"Syntometrine contains ergometrine, so it is also contraindicated.\",\"Carboprost (15-methyl PGF2α) causes bronchospasm, so it is contraindicated in asthma.\",\"Correct. Misoprostol (a PGE1 analogue) has no bronchoconstrictor or hypertensive effect, so it is the safe choice here.\",\"Dinoprostone is used for cervical ripening, not postpartum haemorrhage (PPH).\"],\"e\":[\"Drug sequence for atony: oxytocin, then ergometrine (not if hypertensive), then carboprost (not if asthmatic), then misoprostol.\",\"Tranexamic acid is given in all PPH.\"],\"tk\":\"\",\"pr\":\"\",\"tp\":\"\",\"cn\":\"Uterotonic contraindications: ergometrine in hypertension or pre-eclampsia, carboprost in asthma; misoprostol is safe in both.\",\"sl\":\"Carboprost 0.25 mg IM (use with caution in women with asthma); misoprostol 800 micrograms sublingually.\",\"s\":[[\"L09_0\",[15]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"R10\"},{\"id\":\"R11\",\"k\":\"R\",\"b\":\"hayat\",\"g\":null,\"L\":35,\"ty\":[],\"lv\":null,\"ti\":\"Contraception\",\"st\":\"Contraception\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A couple requests sterilisation. Which statement about vasectomy compared with tubal ligation is correct?\",\"o\":[\"Vasectomy is effective immediately after the procedure\",\"Vasectomy needs general anaesthesia and a laparoscopy\",\"Vasectomy is less invasive, but semen analysis is needed to confirm sterility\",\"Vasectomy has a higher failure rate than tubal ligation\",\"Vasectomy is easily reversed in most men\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Sperm remain in the distal vas for weeks, so other contraception is needed until azoospermia is confirmed.\",\"Vasectomy is done under local anaesthetic; tubal ligation needs laparoscopy, usually under general anaesthesia.\",\"Correct. Vasectomy is simpler, safer and cheaper than tubal ligation, but it is not effective until semen analysis shows no sperm (about 12 weeks later).\",\"Vasectomy has a lower failure rate (about 1 in 2000) than tubal occlusion (about 1 in 200).\",\"Both methods should be counselled as permanent.\"],\"e\":[\"Counsel both partners that sterilisation is permanent.\",\"Vasectomy: about 1 in 2000 failure, needs post-procedure semen analysis.\",\"Tubal occlusion: about 1 in 200 lifetime failure, effective immediately, risk of ectopic if it fails.\"],\"tk\":\"\",\"pr\":\"\",\"tp\":\"\",\"cn\":\"Vasectomy: local anaesthetic, lower failure, but contraception is needed until semen analysis confirms azoospermia.\",\"sl\":\"Male sterilisation: vasectomy.\",\"s\":[[\"L35_0\",[38,58]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"R11\"},{\"id\":\"R12\",\"k\":\"R\",\"b\":\"hayat\",\"g\":null,\"L\":35,\"ty\":[],\"lv\":null,\"ti\":\"Contraception\",\"st\":\"Contraception\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A breastfeeding woman wants contraception. Which method is suitable?\",\"o\":[\"Combined oral contraceptive pill\",\"Combined vaginal ring\",\"Progestogen-only method\",\"Combined transdermal patch\",\"Combined injectable contraceptive\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Oestrogen reduces milk supply and is UKMEC 4 in the first 6 weeks postpartum while breastfeeding.\",\"The ring contains oestrogen, same problem.\",\"Correct. Progestogen-only methods (pill, implant, injection, levonorgestrel intrauterine system [LNG-IUS]) do not affect breastfeeding and can be started early.\",\"The patch contains oestrogen.\",\"Combined injectables contain oestrogen.\"],\"e\":[\"Every option except one contained oestrogen; the odd one out was the answer.\",\"Lactational amenorrhoea is reliable only if fully breastfeeding, amenorrhoeic and under 6 months postpartum.\"],\"tk\":\"\",\"pr\":\"\",\"tp\":\"\",\"cn\":\"Breastfeeding: use progestogen-only methods; avoid combined (oestrogen-containing) methods, especially in the first 6 weeks.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"R12\"},{\"id\":\"R13\",\"k\":\"R\",\"b\":\"hayat\",\"g\":null,\"L\":35,\"ty\":[],\"lv\":null,\"ti\":\"Contraception\",\"st\":\"Contraception\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which is the most effective emergency contraception?\",\"o\":[\"Levonorgestrel 1.5 mg\",\"Ulipristal acetate 30 mg\",\"Copper intrauterine device (IUD)\",\"Combined pills (Yuzpe method)\",\"Levonorgestrel 3 mg\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Levonorgestrel works up to 72 hours and is less effective than the copper IUD.\",\"Ulipristal works up to 120 hours and is the most effective oral method, but less effective than the IUD.\",\"Correct. The copper IUD, inserted up to 5 days after unprotected sex (or 5 days after expected ovulation), has a failure rate below 1% and gives ongoing contraception.\",\"The Yuzpe method is outdated and the least effective.\",\"A double dose of levonorgestrel is used with enzyme-inducing drugs or body mass index (BMI) over 26; it is still less effective than the IUD.\"],\"e\":[\"Emergency contraception, most to least effective: copper IUD, ulipristal, levonorgestrel.\",\"Copper IUD also gives ongoing contraception.\"],\"tk\":\"\",\"pr\":\"\",\"tp\":\"\",\"cn\":\"Copper intrauterine device (IUD) is the most effective emergency contraception (failure below 1%), up to 5 days after intercourse.\",\"sl\":\"Copper IUCD: failure rate below 2% (0.2–2 per 100 woman-years); copper is toxic to sperm and prevents implantation.\",\"s\":[[\"L35_0\",[42]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"R13\"},{\"id\":\"R14\",\"k\":\"R\",\"b\":\"hayat\",\"g\":null,\"L\":35,\"ty\":[],\"lv\":null,\"ti\":\"Contraception\",\"st\":\"Contraception\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which is a recognised feature of depot medroxyprogesterone acetate (DMPA)?\",\"o\":[\"It contains oestrogen\",\"It increases bone mineral density\",\"Delayed return of fertility after stopping\",\"It must be given every month\",\"It causes heavy regular periods\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"DMPA is progestogen-only.\",\"DMPA lowers bone mineral density (reversible on stopping).\",\"Correct. Dr. Fida's slides: delayed return of fertility, up to 18 months after the last injection, so DMPA is not ideal for women who plan pregnancy soon.\",\"It is given every 12 weeks (12-weekly in the slides).\",\"It causes irregular bleeding at first and amenorrhoea in many women with longer use.\"],\"e\":[\"DMPA side effects: irregular bleeding then amenorrhoea, weight gain, reduced bone density, delayed return of fertility.\"],\"tk\":\"\",\"pr\":\"\",\"tp\":\"\",\"cn\":\"Depot medroxyprogesterone acetate (DMPA): delayed return of fertility (up to 18 months), small reversible reduction in bone density, weight gain, given 12-weekly.\",\"sl\":\"Depot medroxyprogesterone acetate (DMPA) 12-weekly; delayed return of fertility up to 18 months; small reversible reduction in bone mineral density.\",\"s\":[[\"L35_0\",[35,36]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"R14\"},{\"id\":\"R15\",\"k\":\"R\",\"b\":\"hayat\",\"g\":null,\"L\":35,\"ty\":[],\"lv\":null,\"ti\":\"Contraception\",\"st\":\"Contraception\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"What is the commonest side effect of the etonogestrel subdermal implant?\",\"o\":[\"Weight loss\",\"Irregular, unpredictable bleeding\",\"Deep vein thrombosis\",\"Delayed return of fertility for a year\",\"Heavy regular periods\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Weight change is reported but is not the commonest problem.\",\"Correct. Unpredictable bleeding is the commonest side effect and the main reason women remove the implant.\",\"Progestogen-only methods do not carry the oestrogen-related thrombosis risk.\",\"Fertility returns quickly after removal (unlike depot medroxyprogesterone acetate [DMPA]).\",\"Bleeding is irregular; amenorrhoea is also common.\"],\"e\":[\"The implant lasts 3 years and is one of the most effective methods (failure under 1 in 1000).\"],\"tk\":\"\",\"pr\":\"\",\"tp\":\"\",\"cn\":\"Implant: commonest side effect is irregular unpredictable bleeding; fertility returns promptly after removal.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"r\",\"n\":\"The student recalled that a question on implants was asked but not its wording, so this question was rebuilt around the most likely point.\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"R15\"},{\"id\":\"R16\",\"k\":\"R\",\"b\":\"hayat\",\"g\":null,\"L\":10,\"ty\":[],\"lv\":null,\"ti\":\"Diabetes in pregnancy\",\"st\":\"Diabetes in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 75 g oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT) at 26 weeks gives: fasting 5.3 mmol/L (95 mg/dL), 1 hour 9.0 mmol/L, 2 hours 7.6 mmol/L. What is the interpretation?\",\"o\":[\"Normal, because only one value is abnormal\",\"Gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM), because the fasting value is 5.1 mmol/L or more\",\"Impaired glucose tolerance; repeat the test in 4 weeks\",\"Overt diabetes\",\"Normal, because the 2-hour value is below 8.5 mmol/L\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Under the IADPSG criteria, one abnormal value is enough.\",\"Correct. IADPSG thresholds are fasting 5.1, 1 hour 10.0, 2 hours 8.5 mmol/L (92, 180, 153 mg/dL); any one value at or above them diagnoses GDM.\",\"There is no 'impaired tolerance' category in pregnancy under IADPSG; a single abnormal value is GDM.\",\"Overt diabetes needs fasting of 7.0 mmol/L or more, or random 11.1 or more.\",\"A normal 2-hour value does not cancel the abnormal fasting value.\"],\"e\":[\"92 mg/dL (5.1 mmol/L) is the diagnostic fasting cut-off.\",\"The treatment target is a different number; see the next question.\"],\"tk\":\"\",\"pr\":\"\",\"tp\":\"\",\"cn\":\"IADPSG 75 g OGTT: fasting ≥ 5.1, 1 h ≥ 10.0, 2 h ≥ 8.5 mmol/L; one value is enough for gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM).\",\"sl\":\"Interpretation of the 75 g OGTT according to the International Association of Diabetes and Pregnancy Study Groups (IADPSG).\",\"s\":[[\"L10_0\",[30]]],\"f\":\"r\",\"n\":\"The student recalled that oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT) values were given but not the numbers, so the values were rebuilt.\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"R16\"},{\"id\":\"R17\",\"k\":\"R\",\"b\":\"hayat\",\"g\":null,\"L\":10,\"ty\":[],\"lv\":null,\"ti\":\"Diabetes in pregnancy\",\"st\":\"Diabetes in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A woman with gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) remains above target on metformin despite diet. What is the next step?\",\"o\":[\"Increase dietary restriction further\",\"Add glibenclamide\",\"Start insulin\",\"Induce labour now\",\"Stop metformin and observe\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Diet has already failed; further restriction risks ketosis and poor fetal growth.\",\"Sulfonylureas are not the standard next step in the slides.\",\"Correct. When targets are not met on diet and metformin, insulin is added (or started if metformin is not tolerated).\",\"Delivery timing is decided by control and complications, not as the next step for hyperglycaemia.\",\"Uncontrolled glucose needs treatment, not observation.\"],\"e\":[\"Insulin is also the first choice when fasting glucose at diagnosis is 7.0 mmol/L or more.\"],\"tk\":\"\",\"pr\":\"\",\"tp\":\"\",\"cn\":\"Gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM): diet and exercise, then metformin, then insulin when targets are not met.\",\"sl\":\"If targets are not met with diet and exercise in 1–2 weeks, offer metformin; if not met with diet, exercise and metformin, offer insulin as well.\",\"s\":[[\"L10_0\",[32,33]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"R17\"},{\"id\":\"R18\",\"k\":\"R\",\"b\":\"hayat\",\"g\":null,\"L\":10,\"ty\":[],\"lv\":null,\"ti\":\"Diabetes in pregnancy\",\"st\":\"Diabetes in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"What is the fasting glucose target on treatment for gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM), according to Dr. Hadeel's slides?\",\"o\":[\"Below 5.0 mmol/L (about 90 mg/dL)\",\"Below 5.5 mmol/L (about 100 mg/dL)\",\"Below 6.0 mmol/L (about 108 mg/dL)\",\"Below 7.0 mmol/L (126 mg/dL)\",\"Below 7.8 mmol/L (140 mg/dL)\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Correct. The slides give treatment targets of fasting below 5 mmol/L and 2 hours after meals below 7 mmol/L.\",\"Too high for the slides' target.\",\"Too high.\",\"This is the fasting threshold for overt diabetes, not a target.\",\"This is a 1-hour post-meal figure used in some guidelines, not a fasting target.\"],\"e\":[\"Students in Hayat picked 92 mg/dL, the lowest option offered. 92 mg/dL (5.1 mmol/L) is the IADPSG diagnostic cut-off, not the treatment target. Learn the slide numbers.\"],\"tk\":\"\",\"pr\":\"\",\"tp\":\"\",\"cn\":\"Gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) treatment targets (slides): fasting < 5 mmol/L, 2 hours after meals < 7 mmol/L.\",\"sl\":\"Treatment goal: target blood glucose fasting < 5 mmol/L, 2 hours < 7 mmol/L.\",\"s\":[[\"L10_0\",[17]]],\"f\":\"r\",\"n\":\"The student recalled choosing '< 92 mg/dL' (the lowest option offered). The options were rebuilt around the slide target.\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"R18\"},{\"id\":\"R19\",\"k\":\"R\",\"b\":\"hayat\",\"g\":null,\"L\":37,\"ty\":[],\"lv\":null,\"ti\":\"Pelvic organ prolapse\",\"st\":\"Pelvic organ prolapse\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"On Pelvic Organ Prolapse Quantification (POP-Q) examination the leading edge of the anterior wall is 3 cm beyond the hymen, and total vaginal length is 9 cm. What is the stage?\",\"o\":[\"Stage I\",\"Stage II\",\"Stage III\",\"Stage IV\",\"Stage 0\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Stage I: the most distal part is more than 1 cm above the hymen.\",\"Stage II: the most distal part is within 1 cm above or below the hymen.\",\"Correct. Stage III: more than 1 cm below the hymen but not more than (total vaginal length − 2) cm, here between +1 and +7 cm.\",\"Stage IV: complete eversion, at least (total vaginal length − 2) cm beyond the hymen.\",\"Stage 0: no prolapse.\"],\"e\":[\"The hymen is the reference point (0).\",\"Points above it are negative, points below are positive.\"],\"tk\":\"\",\"pr\":\"\",\"tp\":\"\",\"cn\":\"Pelvic Organ Prolapse Quantification (POP-Q) stages: I above −1, II within ±1 cm of hymen, III beyond +1 but short of TVL−2, IV complete eversion.\",\"sl\":\"New classification: the POP-Q system (International Continence Society 1996, Bump et al.): site-specific and quantitative.\",\"s\":[[\"L37_0\",[41]]],\"f\":\"r\",\"n\":\"A prolapse case was asked but no student recalled the questions, so this question was rebuilt from Dr. Hamzah's staging slides.\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"R19\"},{\"id\":\"R20\",\"k\":\"R\",\"b\":\"hayat\",\"g\":null,\"L\":37,\"ty\":[],\"lv\":null,\"ti\":\"Pelvic organ prolapse\",\"st\":\"Pelvic organ prolapse\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 68-year-old woman with symptomatic stage II prolapse is not fit for, and does not want, surgery. What is the best option?\",\"o\":[\"Vaginal hysterectomy\",\"Vaginal pessary\",\"Sacrocolpopexy\",\"Observation only\",\"Colpocleisis\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Surgery has been declined and she is unfit.\",\"Correct. A ring or other pessary, with pelvic floor muscle training and vaginal oestrogen, is the conservative treatment for symptomatic prolapse.\",\"This is an abdominal operation.\",\"Symptomatic prolapse deserves treatment if the patient wishes.\",\"Colpocleisis is an operation, even if minor.\"],\"e\":[\"Pessary complications: discharge, ulceration; review every 6 months.\"],\"tk\":\"\",\"pr\":\"\",\"tp\":\"\",\"cn\":\"Conservative management of pelvic organ prolapse (POP): pelvic floor muscle training, pessary (with vaginal oestrogen), lifestyle measures.\",\"sl\":\"Options of management: no treatment (pelvic floor exercise); conservative, such as physiotherapy or pessary; surgical treatment.\",\"s\":[[\"L37_0\",[47]]],\"f\":\"r\",\"n\":\"Rebuilt: the station topic is from Hayat's paper, the question is a likely one.\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"R20\"},{\"id\":\"R21\",\"k\":\"R\",\"b\":\"hayat\",\"g\":null,\"L\":19,\"ty\":[],\"lv\":null,\"ti\":\"Oral · Preterm labour\",\"st\":\"Oral · Preterm labour\",\"c\":\"G3 P2+1 at 29 weeks presents in preterm labour with intact membranes.\",\"q\":\"Which drug gives fetal neuroprotection?\",\"o\":[\"Betamethasone\",\"Magnesium sulfate\",\"Nifedipine\",\"Progesterone\",\"Atosiban\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Corticosteroids mature the lungs and reduce intraventricular haemorrhage, but neuroprotection is the role of magnesium.\",\"Correct. Magnesium sulfate given before 30 weeks (consider up to 34 weeks) when birth is expected within 24 hours reduces cerebral palsy.\",\"Nifedipine is a tocolytic.\",\"Vaginal progesterone is used for prevention in women with a short cervix, not in established labour.\",\"Atosiban is a tocolytic.\"],\"e\":[\"Order in the slides: hydration, corticosteroids, magnesium sulfate, tocolysis, antibiotics for group B streptococcus (GBS) as indicated, transfer to a unit with a NICU.\"],\"tk\":\"\",\"pr\":\"\",\"tp\":\"\",\"cn\":\"Magnesium sulfate before 30 weeks (consider up to 34) gives fetal neuroprotection against cerebral palsy.\",\"sl\":\"Magnesium sulphate given to mothers shortly before delivery reduces the risk of cerebral palsy and protects gross motor function in infants born preterm.\",\"s\":[[\"L19_0\",[37]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"R21\"},{\"id\":\"R22\",\"k\":\"R\",\"b\":\"hayat\",\"g\":null,\"L\":19,\"ty\":[],\"lv\":null,\"ti\":\"Oral · Preterm labour\",\"st\":\"Oral · Preterm labour\",\"c\":\"G3 P2+1 at 29 weeks presents in preterm labour with intact membranes.\",\"q\":\"What is the corticosteroid regimen?\",\"o\":[\"Dexamethasone 6 mg orally once\",\"Betamethasone 12 mg intramuscular (IM), two doses 24 hours apart\",\"Hydrocortisone 100 mg intravenous (IV) every 6 hours\",\"Prednisolone 40 mg orally daily for 5 days\",\"Betamethasone 24 mg IM weekly until 34 weeks\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Antenatal steroids are given intramuscularly; dexamethasone is 6 mg IM every 12 hours for 4 doses.\",\"Correct. Betamethasone 12 mg intramuscularly, two doses 24 hours apart (or dexamethasone 6 mg IM 12-hourly × 4).\",\"Hydrocortisone does not cross the placenta well enough.\",\"Prednisolone is metabolised by the placenta and does not reach the fetus.\",\"Weekly repeated courses are not recommended.\"],\"e\":[\"Steroids reduce respiratory distress syndrome, intraventricular haemorrhage and neonatal death; the maximum benefit is 24 hours to 7 days after the first dose.\"],\"tk\":\"\",\"pr\":\"\",\"tp\":\"\",\"cn\":\"Antenatal steroids: betamethasone 12 mg intramuscular (IM) × 2, 24 hours apart, between 24 and 34+6 weeks.\",\"sl\":\"Corticosteroids decrease respiratory distress syndrome (RDS), neonatal mortality and intraventricular haemorrhage; offer at 24+0 to 34+6 weeks in suspected or diagnosed preterm labour.\",\"s\":[[\"L19_0\",[31,32]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"R22\"},{\"id\":\"R23\",\"k\":\"R\",\"b\":\"hayat\",\"g\":null,\"L\":19,\"ty\":[],\"lv\":null,\"ti\":\"Oral · Preterm labour\",\"st\":\"Oral · Preterm labour\",\"c\":\"G3 P2+1 at 29 weeks presents in preterm labour with intact membranes.\",\"q\":\"Which is a recommended first-line tocolytic?\",\"o\":[\"Salbutamol infusion\",\"Nifedipine\",\"Oxytocin\",\"Misoprostol\",\"Vaginal progesterone\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Beta-agonists are no longer recommended because of maternal side effects (tachycardia, pulmonary oedema).\",\"Correct. Nifedipine (or atosiban) is used to delay delivery by 48 hours so steroids can work and the woman can be transferred.\",\"Oxytocin causes contractions.\",\"Misoprostol causes contractions.\",\"Progesterone is used for prevention, not to stop established labour.\"],\"e\":[\"Contraindications to tocolysis: chorioamnionitis, fetal compromise, abruption, lethal anomaly, advanced labour.\"],\"tk\":\"\",\"pr\":\"\",\"tp\":\"\",\"cn\":\"Tocolysis (nifedipine or atosiban) buys 48 hours for steroids and transfer; it does not improve neonatal outcome by itself.\",\"sl\":\"Tocolytics buy time until steroids work and allow in-utero transfer; offer between 26 and 33+6 weeks. Nifedipine is first line (but not given with magnesium sulfate [MgSO4]); atosiban is an oxytocin antagonist.\",\"s\":[[\"L19_0\",[35,36]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"R23\"}]}";